const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "E", "2": "C", "3": "A", "4": "E", "5": "B", "6": "A", "7": "B", "8": "D", "9": "E", "10": "A", "11": "B", "12": "D", "13": "B", "14": "C", "15": "A", "16": "F", "17": "E", "18": "C", "19": "B", "20": "C", "21": "F", "22": "D", "23": "B", "24": "A", "25": "E", "26": "D", "27": "A", "28": "F", "29": "E", "30": "A", "31": "D", "32": "F", "33": "B", "34": "E", "35": "D", "36": "E", "37": "B", "38": "A", "39": "E", "40": "B", "41": "D", "42": "A", "43": "E", "44": "C", "45": "B", "46": "E", "47": "B", "48": "A", "49": "D", "50": "B"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most appropriate next step in this patient's management is reassurance only, given nocturnal enuresis is considered normal child behavior up until age 5 years. This management approach is supported by the absence of other concerning symptoms, as well as the patient meeting normal developmental milestones of a 3-year-old boy.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Normal child development is evidenced by an absence of any red flag signs or symptoms in a child that is meeting their developmental milestones. Enuresis is normal at age 3 years, and, in the absence of abnormal developmental, historical, or examination features, is not a cause for alarm.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Normal child development is the growth and gradual acquisition of skills over an age-appropriate timeframe. Enuresis is a normal feature of growth and development until age 5 years. A child older than 5 years may be diagnosed with primary or secondary enuresis. Primary enuresis is due to a delay in maturation, while secondary is caused by underlying medical conditions or stressors.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no known prevention for primary enuresis aside from preventative healthcare to promote normal development.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Enuresis related to normal child development will generally resolve with time and reassurance. Patients with enuresis should undergo a thorough history, physical examination, and relevant laboratory study including urinalysis to rule out medical causes or stressors as the cause. While many cases of primary enuresis spontaneously resolve, first-line therapies include motivational therapy or enuresis alarms. Medication therapies, such as desmopressin, imipramine, or oxybutynin, are options if conservative management strategies fail.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient is younger than 5 years, presents in the absence of red flag signs/symptoms, and has been meeting all of his developmental milestones. If he were older, the most likely diagnosis would be primary enuresis. Pathologic enuresis would be considered if the patient were not meeting his developmental milestones or had red flag signs/symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choices A, C, and D:</b> Desmopressin therapy, imipramine therapy, and oxybutynin therapy are incorrect. There is no need to treat this patient's nocturnal enuresis, given that it is typical of normal child development and will likely resolve over time. If this patient were aged 5 years or older, it would be best to start with conservative treatment, such as an enuresis alarm or motivational therapy.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Enuresis alarm is incorrect. An enuresis alarm would be a reasonable next step if the patient were aged 5 years or older, at which point his presentation would be considered primary enuresis.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has delusional disorder. Delusional disorder is characterized by the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer without other psychotic symptoms. Delusional parasitosis, as seen in this patient, is a subtype of delusional disorder in which individuals rigidly believe they are infested with parasitic organisms.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Delusional disorder is characterized by the presence of one or more delusions (ie, fixed, false beliefs) for 1 month or longer without other psychotic symptoms (eg, hallucinations). The disorder does not typically affect an individual's daily functioning.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Delusional parasitosis is a subtype of delusional disorder in which individuals rigidly believe they are infested with parasites, mites, worms, or other organisms. Delusional parasitosis is typically related to an underlying psychiatric disorder (eg, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder), though some secondary causes include neuropathies, neurologic conditions, such as strokes, stimulant intoxication, infections (eg, leprosy), or adverse effects of medication. These secondary causes commonly involve sensory disturbances that the patient misinterprets as infestation.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Given the variety of causes, there is no universal strategy for prevention of delusional parasitosis or delusional disorders generally.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Physicians should attempt to build a therapeutic alliance, including acknowledging the patient's distressing symptoms. Though many patients reject a psychological explanation, antipsychotics should be offered.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Illness anxiety disorder and body dysmorphic disorder both present with beliefs or obsessions related to an individual's body. However, in illness anxiety disorder, the patient is mostly preoccupied with the possibility that they may have a serious or life-threatening illness (rather than verbalizing a specific belief about infestation) and may experience transient reassurance with negative test results. Body dysmorphic disorder manifests as preoccupation with a perceived flaw in physical appearance. Patients with delusional parasitosis are preoccupied by infestation rather than appearance flaws.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Body dysmorphic disorder is incorrect. Body dysmorphic disorder manifests as preoccupation with a perceived flaw in physical appearance leading to repetitive behaviors (eg, checking) or mental acts (eg, comparing appearance to others). Patients with delusional parasitosis are preoccupied by infestation, not appearance.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Conversion disorder is incorrect. Conversion disorder, also known as functional neurological symptom disorder, involves neurologic symptoms, such as sensory or motor dysfunction, that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging. Patients with functional neurological symptom disorder do not espouse rigid, false beliefs about the cause of their symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Illness anxiety disorder (hypochondriasis) is incorrect. Patients with illness anxiety disorder demonstrate persistently excessive anxiety and preoccupation about the possibility that they may have or may acquire a serious illness, such that they perform frequent health-related behaviors or exhibit maladaptive avoidance. A specific rigid belief about infestation is more consistent with delusional parasitosis.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Unspecified delirium is incorrect. Delirium presents with acute disturbances in awareness, attention, and baseline cognition that fluctuate in severity over the course of the day. Other than her infestation beliefs, this patient is cognitively intact.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient is most likely experiencing acute mania with psychosis. She also has a history of depressive episodes. Patients who experience acute mania and depression meet criteria for bipolar disorder.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder characterized by episodes of elevated mood (mania or hypomania) and depression, with significant functional impairment. This patient is currently in a manic episode, as evidenced by her grandiose thinking and irresponsible spending, with a history of depression evidenced by her weeklong periods of isolation and decreased eating. In severe cases, patients can experience psychosis, such as this patient's internal stimulation.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Diagnosis of bipolar disorder excludes substance-induced or medical causes. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric (and at times labile) mood, increased energy, decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and/or grandiose thinking. Experiencing acute mania qualifies a patient for a bipolar disorder diagnosis. Hypomanic episodes feature fewer and less severe manic symptoms compared to acute mania. Depressive symptoms include depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, suicidal thoughts, psychomotor slowing, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (ie, decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance).</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause of bipolar disorder is unknown, there is no universal way to prevent bipolar disorder.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Mood-stabilizing medication is used to treat acute mood episodes and prevent future mood episodes.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The differential diagnosis for bipolar disorder commonly includes borderline personality disorder and schizoaffective disorder. In borderline personality disorder, mood changes typically occur over hours rather than days to weeks. In schizoaffective disorder, discrete mood episodes may be present, though patients also experience isolated psychotic symptoms in the absence of mood symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Brief psychotic episode is incorrect. Brief psychotic disorder is characterized by at least one acute psychotic symptom (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month. This patient is experiencing manic symptoms in conjunction with her psychotic symptoms, and has experienced similar episodes in the past, which is more consistent with bipolar disorder.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Delusional disorder is incorrect. Delusional disorder is characterized by the presence of one or more delusions (ie, fixed, false beliefs) for 1 month or longer without other psychotic symptoms, such as hallucinations. This patient is experiencing manic symptoms in conjunction with her psychotic symptoms (her internal stimulation indicates she may be experiencing hallucinations). This presentation is most consistent with bipolar disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder is incorrect. Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder presents with irritability and angry outbursts. The course is typically chronic rather than episodic. Also, this patient demonstrates psychotic symptoms and additional mood symptoms consistent with bipolar disorder.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Schizophrenia is incorrect. Schizophrenia disorder involves 6 months or longer of two of the following: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or disorganized behavior (known as positive symptoms), or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). The course is typically progressive. Discrete episodes of behavior change are more consistent with bipolar disorder. Also, she demonstrates manic symptoms in conjunction with psychotic symptoms, indicating that her psychotic symptoms are likely secondary to her mood disorder.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presents with a history of multiple somatic symptoms, related distress, and healthcare seeking behaviors, consistent with somatic symptom disorder. In somatic symptom disorder, the patient is excessively preoccupied with one or more somatic symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt daily life.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with somatic symptom disorder persistently devote excessive time and energy to symptoms or related health concerns (eg, repeatedly going to the doctor). Somatic symptom disorder typically has a chronic and fluctuating course.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Patients with somatic symptom disorder experience excessive concern about one or more physical symptoms, to the extent that these symptoms interfere with daily functioning. These symptoms may or may not originate from an underlying illness though are typically unconsciously produced or exacerbated by psychological factors, such as stress, as seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause is unknown, there is no universal prevention strategy for somatic symptom disorder.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management of somatic symptom disorder centers around regular primary care follow-up that targets coping skills, provides reassurance, and avoids unnecessary tests.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In factitious disorder, patients consciously exaggerate, produce, or fabricate symptoms. Though factitious disorder may be on the differential diagnosis for a patient with multiple invasive procedures despite no structural symptom cause, there is no evidence that this patient is falsifying or inducing her symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Body dysmorphic disorder is incorrect. Body dysmorphic disorder manifests as preoccupation with a perceived flaw in physical appearance leading to repetitive behaviors (eq, checking) or mental acts (eg, comparing appearance to others). This patient is preoccupied with somatic symptoms rather than a flaw in her appearance.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Conversion disorder is incorrect. Functional neurological symptom disorder (conversion disorder) involves neurologic symptoms, such as sensory or motor dysfunction, that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Factitious disorder is incorrect. In factitious disorder, patients consciously produce symptoms (eg, purposely injure themselves, fake a fever by eating something warm) for primary gain. This patient's somatic symptoms are not purely neurologic, as the symptoms span body systems, and there is no evidence that this patient is falsifying or inducing her symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Psychological factors affecting another medical condition is incorrect. Psychological factors affecting another medical condition refers to any mental factor (eg, distress, maladaptive coping, illness denial, health risk behaviors) that inhibits recovery or treatment from a medical condition. This patient's distress is leading her to seek care excessively but is not clearly exacerbating an underlying health condition.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient demonstrates impairment specific to reading, consistent with developmental reading disorder (also known as dyslexia). Dyslexia involves chronic phonologic processing dysfunction that manifests with reading difficulties and consequent poor academic or occupational performance.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Dyslexia is a specific learning disorder involving dysfunctional phonologic processing that manifests with reading difficulties and consequent poor academic or occupational performance. These learning difficulties cannot be attributed to global mental conditions, such as intellectual disability, sensory impairment, or other psychiatric or neurologic disorders.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Specific learning disorders are neurodevelopmental conditions associated with impaired function in reading (dyslexia), writing (dysgraphia), or mathematics (dyscalculia). Diagnosis of dyslexia requires a comprehensive assessment involving standardized testing, cognitive evaluations, and clinical judgment.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no universal prevention strategy, though avoiding risk factors, such as environmental toxins, may help.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Individuals with specific learning disorders benefit from longitudinal assessment and individualized education plans.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Dyslexia is a specific learning disorder caused by cognitive deficits in basic reading skills and should be differentiated from intellectual disabilities and secondary reading problems, such as hearing or vision impairment.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Autism spectrum disorder is incorrect. Autism spectrum disorder is characterized by social communication deficits, repetitive behaviors, restricted interests, and impaired social interaction. This patient does not demonstrate clear social deficits.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is incorrect. Social anxiety disorder refers to excessive fear of scrutiny or embarrassment in social and/or performance situations. Though social anxiety disorder may inhibit a patient's academic performance, this impairment would be global rather than specific to reading.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Specific expressive language disorder is incorrect. Specific expressive language disorder is a communication disorder presenting with difficulties producing spoken language, not in understanding receptive (ie, written) language.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Normal development is incorrect. This patient consistently underperforms on academic tasks involving reading, making dyslexia the most likely diagnosis.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient does not view his alcohol use as a problem, instead rationalizing his drinking behavior as treating his anxiety. This indicates he is in the precontemplation stage of behavioral change.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> The stages of behavioral change are used to define a patient's readiness to change a health-related behavior, such as substance use, diet, or exercise habits.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Patients in the precontemplation stage are not interested in changing their habit and/or may not see their detrimental habit as a problem. Physicians aim to move patients through the stages of behavioral change over time with an interview technique called motivational interviewing. Motivational interviewing involves using open-ended, nonjudgmental questions to help the patient explore their reasons for wanting to change or maintain the habit.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Contemplation is the next stage in which patients are ambivalent about changing their habit.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choices B, C, and D:</b> Contemplation, preparation, and action are incorrect. Patients in the contemplation stage may view their behavior as a problem but be unsure about whether they want to change. Patients in the preparation stage have committed to making a change and are ready to discuss strategies and resources to help them make the change. In the action stage, patients start to take steps to change their behavior. This patient does not view his drinking as a problem and is therefore not interested in changing his habit, putting him currently in the precontemplation stage.</p><p><b>Choice E and Choice F:</b> Maintenance and relapse prevention are incorrect. After making the change, the patient must maintain the change and strategize how to cope with temptation to return to the old habit, preventing relapse. This patient has not changed his drinking behavior and is currently not interested in changing his habit.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's evolving symptoms of dry, flushed skin, hyperthermia, tachycardia, hypertension, mydriasis, and confusion in the setting of haloperidol and benztropine use are consistent with anticholinergic delirium, which results from blockade of acetylcholine receptors.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Anticholinergic toxicity is a clinical diagnosis and should be suspected in a patient experiencing symptoms such as delirium, relative adrenergic/sympathomimetic excess (eg, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension, hyperthermia, tremors, agitation), dry mucous membranes, flushed skin without sweating, visual disturbance, ileus/constipation, and urinary retention, among others, following administration of medications known to block acetylcholine receptors.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Endogenous acetylcholine binding to muscarinic receptors causes smooth muscle contraction of the sweat glands, salivary glands, airways, bladder, and bowel. Cholinergic neurons projecting to the cerebral cortex mediate attention, learning, and memory. Antagonism of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors blocks cholinergic signaling in the brain and causes the dysfunction of smooth muscle contraction, leading to cognitive impairment, disorientation, delirium, sedation, mydriasis, anhidrosis (leading to mild hyperthermia and flushed skin in this patient), dry mouth, urinary retention, and/or constipation. Many medications block acetylcholine receptors, with some common examples including tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, and diphenhydramine.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Knowledge and avoidance of anticholinergic medications is the mainstay of prevention, though prevention may not always be possible. Patients may require administration of anticholinergic medications for their safety or for appropriate treatment; thus, the benefits of administration may outweigh the risks. Patients receiving anticholinergic medications should be monitored for signs of anticholinergic toxidromes, which should also be treated if they occur.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of anticholinergic toxidrome includes discontinuing the offending medications and providing pharmacotherapy such as benzodiazepines, which balance the state of relative sympathetic excess in anticholinergic toxicity. Supportive care with intravenous fluids, antipyretics, and sedatives is also appropriate depending on the severity of symptoms. In extreme cases, sedation with intubation may be required while the patient metabolizes the medication. Physostigmine can be used in select cases; however, the appropriate dose to counterbalance the anticholinergic effect may be imprecise or difficult to calculate with accuracy, risking consequent cholinergic excess.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's sympathetic excess plus signs and symptoms suggestive of acetylcholine deficiency and use of triggering medications narrows the differential diagnosis to anticholinergic delirium. Differentials such as amphetamine-induced psychosis or neuroleptic malignant syndrome would both present without dry skin, and psychotic symptoms or muscular rigidity would be present, respectively.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Amphetamine-induced psychosis is not the most likely diagnosis. Amphetamines are sympathomimetics, and intoxication is characterized by euphoria, grandiosity, increased wakefulness and attention, agitation, anorexia, and paranoia, which are not demonstrated in this case. On examination, patients can present with signs of sympathomimetic toxicity, including hypertension, tachycardia, tachypnea, diaphoresis, and dilated pupils.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Hallucinogen intoxication delirium is not the most likely diagnosis. In cases of hallucinogen intoxication delirium (such as with cannabis or LSD use), patients typically present with hallucinations as a prominent presenting symptom without signs of anticholinergic toxidrome, such as hyperthermia, dry, flushed skin, or cycloplegia.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Major depressive disorder is incorrect. Major depressive disorder presents with depressive symptoms, anhedonia, guilt, low energy, concentration changes, appetite changes, sleep disturbances, and psychomotor slowing, and may demonstrate psychotic or suicidal features. Autonomic instability, such as hyperthermia, tachycardia, and hypertension, would be atypical.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is incorrect. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome presents with muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, tachycardia, and hypertension in the setting of antipsychotic use. This patient has a normal neurologic examination, which would not be the case in neuroleptic malignant syndrome, as an abnormal motor examination due to rigidity is a defining feature.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Schizophrenia, paranoid type, is incorrect. Patients with schizophrenia, paranoid type, present with paranoia, delusions, disorganized speech, and disorganized thinking, along with negative symptoms, such as poor hygiene. This patient lacks characteristic features of an exacerbation of schizophrenia and has autonomic instability, which would not occur as a primary feature of schizophrenia.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, with headaches, fatigue, trouble waking, and enlarged tonsils, likely has obstructive sleep apnea hypopnea (OSAH). The most common cause of pediatric OSAH is tonsillar and adenoid hypertrophy.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> OSAH is common in the pediatric population and often presents with excessive daytime sleepiness, headaches, and snoring.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> In obstructive sleep apnea, transient collapsing of the upper airways during sleep results in hypopnea (partial obstruction) or apnea (complete obstruction). Obstructive sleep apnea in adults is most commonly caused by pharyngeal crowding from obesity, while in pediatric patients, it is most commonly caused by adenotonsillar hypertrophy, particularly in children with a normal BMI.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> While obstructive sleep apnea from obesity can be prevented by maintaining a normal BMI, there is no prevention strategy for OSAH caused by adenotonsillar hypertrophy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> The first-line treatment of pediatric OSAH caused by adenotonsillar hypertrophy is adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy. In some cases, watchful waiting is appropriate if symptoms are mild.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with fatigue, headaches, and large tonsils/adenoids, making the most likely diagnosis OSAH.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Hypothyroidism is incorrect. Hypothyroidism can cause fatigue and depressed mood, cold intolerance, and weight gain. Persistent headaches and large tonsils/adenoids are symptoms more consistent with OSAH, not hypothyroidism.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Major depressive disorder is incorrect. The patient does report feeling down, but this is secondary to the fatigue and symptoms he is experiencing from OSAH. While major depressive disorder can be diagnosed in the presence of another medical illness, the patient does not meet criteria.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Narcolepsy is incorrect. While daytime sleepiness can be seen with narcolepsy, narcolepsy tends to occur suddenly and is associated with cataplexy and hallucinations either before or after sleep. Chronic headaches are not seen in narcolepsy.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is incorrect. A persistent depressive disorder diagnosis requires at least 2 years of depressed mood with symptom-free periods not exceeding 2 months. Depressed mood in this patient is most likely secondary to the fatigue and symptoms he is experiencing from OSAH.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Normal stage of adolescent development is incorrect. While adolescents can present with changes to sleep patterns, such patients should only be symptomatic if the changes lead to inadequate sleep (eg, going to bed at 1 am and waking up at 6 am to go to school). The patient is getting sufficient sleep, and his symptoms are best explained by OSAH.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) withdrawal. Abruptly discontinuing antidepressants with short half-lives, such as paroxetine, can lead to SSRI withdrawal, which manifests as the acute onset of flu-like symptoms, neurologic symptoms, and psychiatric symptoms.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> SSRI withdrawal (also known as antidepressant discontinuation syndrome) manifests as the acute onset of flu-like symptoms, neurologic symptoms (including \"brain zaps\" or electric shock-like symptoms of the head, headache, and paresthesias), and psychiatric symptoms (including irritability, anxiety, and insomnia). Tremors can occasionally occur as well.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Symptoms typically occur within days of abruptly discontinuing an antidepressant that has been taken chronically. Discontinuation of antidepressants with short half- lives, including venlafaxine (a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor) and paroxetine (an SSRI), are most likely to lead to substance withdrawal.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> SSRI withdrawal can be prevented by slowly tapering down the use of antidepressant medications.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Most cases of SSRI withdrawal are mild and self-limited, requiring supportive care only.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Physicians should exclude a worsening of the patient's underlying anxiety disorder. However, anxiety in conjunction with flu-like and neurologic symptoms is most consistent with SSRI withdrawal.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Benign positional vertigo is incorrect. In benign positional vertigo, patients present with minutes-long, abrupt episodes of vertigo that may be accompanied by nausea, Which resolve when holding still. An acute course days after paroxetine discontinuation and associated anxiety are more consistent with SSRI withdrawal.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Functional neurologic symptom disorder is incorrect. Functional neurologic symptom disorder (conversion disorder) involves acute neurologic symptoms, such as sensory or motor dysfunction, that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging. This patient's symptoms and physical examination findings are instead consistent with SSRI withdrawal.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Illness anxiety disorder is incorrect. Illness anxiety disorder presents with persistently excessive anxiety and preoccupation about the possibility that they may have or acquire a serious illness such that they perform frequent health-related behaviors or exhibit maladaptive avoidance. Though this patient appears anxious, she does not report a clear psychological cause of her anxiety and does not demonstrate maladaptive healthcare-seeking behaviors.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Multiple sclerosis is incorrect. Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune demyelinating disease that presents with focal neurologic deficits separated in space and time. This patient's neurologic symptoms do not localize to a particular central nervous system region and are not separated in time. The most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is 'SSRI withdrawal.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Syndrome of inappropriate secretion of ADH (vasopressin) is incorrect. Though it can be an adverse effect of antidepressant medication, syndrome of inappropriate secretion of ADH is asymptomatic unless hyponatremia is severe (unlike this patient's mild hyponatremia), which may present with weakness, headache, seizures, and coma.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Alcohol use is a common cause of erectile dysfunction, affecting the nerves and blood supply to the penis. Continued significant alcohol use and the presence of symptoms during masturbation make alcohol use the most likely cause of his erectile dysfunction.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Difficulty achieving or maintaining an erection during sexual intercourse and masturbation in a male with heavy alcohol use is most consistent with alcohol-induced erectile dysfunction. Alcohol-induced erectile dysfunction can cause distress and interpersonal difficulties within relationships.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Alcohol is a depressant that can damage the neurovascular supply of the penis. Both chronic and acute significant alcohol use can result in erectile dysfunction.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> The best prevention for alcohol induced erectile dysfunction is abstinence from alcohol.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Abstinence or decrease in alcohol use can result in improvements in alcohol-induced erectile dysfunction.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with a 4-week history of erectile dysfunction with sexual intercourse in the setting of chronic significant alcohol use. Although his guilt and anxiety from his sexual performance seems to be significant, he has experienced intermittent erectile dysfunction with masturbation, which makes psychological factors a less likely primary contributor. Significant alcohol use is the most likely explanation for his erectile dysfunction.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B and Choice E:</b> Anxiety about his sexual performance and guilt are incorrect. Psychological factors are a common cause of erectile dysfunction. However, psychological factors only cause erectile dysfunction during sexual intercourse and tend to fluctuate with the mental health of the individual and the stability of the relationship. Psychological factors would not cause erectile dysfunction with masturbation. Alcohol use is more likely in an individual with a history of alcohol use disorder who drinks six beers daily.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Atorvastatin is incorrect. Erectile dysfunction is not a known adverse effect of atorvastatin.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Fluoxetine is not the most likely cause. Fluoxetine can cause delayed ejaculation and erectile dysfunction. However, this patient has been taking fluoxetine for 10 years, and it would be unlikely for this adverse effect to suddenly emerge. In a patient who drinks significant amounts of alcohol, alcohol-induced erectile dysfunction is a more likely diagnosis.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient repeatedly demonstrates an inability to appreciate the extent of his burns and his ability to manage them himself in the home environment. A lack of capacity to refuse treatment can be decided if the patient is unable to clearly articulate an accurate understanding of their medical situation and the likely result of their preferred management.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> A patient with an inability to appreciate the situation and its consequences does not have freedom of decision.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> A patient may lack the capacity to refuse treatment if they cannot explain their medical condition and what would happen if their management strategy were to be pursued. A patient without capacity will present with a fundamental misunderstanding of their medical situation (eg, falsely equating his current serious burn to a minor prior burn as seen in this patient) and demonstrate expectations that are incongruent with reality (eg, expecting to get a nurse at home on his own). There may be a clear reason for this cognitive deficit (eg, a stroke) or not.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The patient's misunderstanding of how serious this burn is, and the belief he does not need more surgery and can get his own nurse to take care of him at home, points to an inability to appreciate the situation and its consequences. He clearly communicates a choice, and while incorrect in his assertions, he does not attempt to fabricate information.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Failure to communicate a choice is incorrect. This patient clearly states that he wants to leave the hospital.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lack of understanding of relevant information is not the most likely indication. This patient understands that he has suffered a serious burn, has undergone extensive treatment, and requires additional treatment. It is his inability to appreciate the severity of this situation and its consequences that most clearly indicates his lack of capacity to refuse treatment.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Poor manipulation of information is not the most likely indication. Poor manipulation of information implies the patient lacks the cognitive ability to receive and interpret information provided by an outside source (eg, for this patient, his care team). He seems to have fundamental misunderstandings of the significance of his condition and does not appreciate the risk; however, he seems to be able to receive and interpret information without issue.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Some patients with social anxiety disorder experience anxiety that is specific to performance situations only. Social anxiety disorder refers to an excessive fear of scrutiny, embarrassment, or rejection in social or performance situations leading to significant distress and/or impaired functioning, as in this patient's case when speaking in front of his attendings and professors.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Social anxiety disorder is characterized by an intense fear of being judged, embarrassed, or rejected in social or performance settings, resulting in significant distress or functional impairment. Patients with social anxiety disorder commonly exhibit shyness and performance anxiety.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Performance anxiety refers to the fear of negative evaluation in social and performance situations, such as public speaking, test-taking, and sexual activity. Some patients with social anxiety disorder experience anxiety that is specific to performance situations only, such as this patient's lack of symptoms with family, friends, or patients.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause is unknown, there is no universal strategy to prevent social anxiety disorder.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> In the performance-only subtype of social anxiety, cognitive behavioral therapy is recommended as the first-line treatment, though as-needed anxiety medications (eg, benzodiazepines, propranolol) are frequently used as well.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Panic attacks typically involve somatic symptoms of anxiety (eg, diaphoresis, dyspnea, paresthesia) and may occur in a variety of anxiety disorders (most commonly panic disorder). Though this patient experiences somatic anxiety symptoms, these symptoms do not meet criteria for a panic attack.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Adjustment disorder with anxiety is incorrect. Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. Patients with adjustment disorder may experience associated depression or anxiety symptoms but do not meet the diagnostic criteria for depression or anxiety disorders. This patient meets criteria for social anxiety disorder.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Generalized anxiety disorder is incorrect. Generalized anxiety disorder is an anxiety disorder that features excessive worrying about a range of topics with associated functional impairment for longer than 6 months. This patient's anxiety is specific to social performance situations.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Panic disorder is incorrect. Panic disorder is characterized by recurrent panic attacks that are unexpected and associated with worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers. This patient's symptoms do not clearly meet full panic attack criteria. As well, his anxiety is expected (Ie, triggered by social performance).</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Specific phobia is incorrect. Specific phobia is a psychiatric disorder featuring anxiety about a certain situation or object that leads to avoidance behavior. This patient's performance anxiety is best characterized by the more specific diagnosis of social anxiety disorder.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), such as citalopram, are a common cause of anorgasmia. Citalopram was initiated 1 month prior to onset of symptoms, making it the most likely cause.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Anorgasmia, or the inability or failure to experience orgasm, is common in women (occurring in about 25% of sexually active women) and frequently multifactorial. Recently initiated SSRIs are a known cause of anorgasmia in the absence of another clear cause.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Anorgasmia can be idiopathic, related to relationship problems, stressors or anxiety, or medications, among other causes. SSRIs are a common cause of anorgasmia.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> While there is no specific way to prevent anorgasmia, choosing medications that are less likely to precipitate anorgasmia when feasible may prevent iatrogenic causes.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Anorgasmia due to a medication can be treated by discontinuing the medication. If medication management of a mood disorder is still required, initiation of medications that are not known to cause sexual dysfunction may be warranted (eg, bupropion).</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with a 2-month history of anorgasmia after starting citalopram 3 months ago. There is no evidence of competing causes of anorgasmia. Amlodipine, omeprazole, and perimenopause do not cause anorgasmia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Amlodipine is incorrect. Amlodipine is not known to cause anorgasmia in women. Side effects of amlodipine include peripheral edema and orthostatic hypotension.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Omeprazole is not the most likely cause. Although there are case reports of omeprazole being associated with anorgasmia, SSRI-induced anorgasmia is common and well-studied.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Perimenopause is incorrect. Menopause can cause vaginal dryness, which could cause difficulty achieving orgasm. However, the patient is perimenopausal (as evidenced by her irregular menstrual cycles) and reports no vaginal dryness or other sexual problems.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Normal response to new relationship status is incorrect. While anorgasmia can occur because of relationship problems, this patient does not report any problems. 'Anorgasmia is not considered a normal response to entering a new relationship, unless coupled with anxiety, for which this patient is currently treated.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has perinatal generalized anxiety disorder, which commonly involves excessive and persistent worrying about fetal, birthing parent, or partner well-being. The most appropriate next step in management is treatment with cognitive behavioral therapy.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Perinatal generalized anxiety disorder commonly involves excessive and persistent worrying about fetal, birthing parent, or partner well-being leading to the disruption of daily functioning (eg, excessive behaviors related to anxiety, work dysfunction). Anxiety can also lead to paresthesia in several body areas, especially in the perioral region and extremities via several mechanisms (eg, muscle tension, attentional bias, etc.).</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Though pregnant patients may not meet the 6-month duration criteria for generalized anxiety disorder, some researchers have advocated for a perinatal generalized anxiety disorder diagnosis with a decreased duration criterion of 1 month, Patients with premorbid psychiatric disorders are vulnerable to peripartum mood and anxiety disorders, such as perinatal generalized anxiety disorder.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Counseling interventions may help prevent peripartum mood and anxiety disorders.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Cognitive behavioral therapy is first-line treatment.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In severe anxiety disorders, antidepressant or anxiolytic medications are commonly added. However, nonpharmacological interventions are preferred in pregnant patients to decrease fetal medication exposure.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Buspirone therapy is incorrect. Buspirone stimulates serotonin receptors and may be useful as a second-line treatment in generalized anxiety disorder. However, cognitive behavioral therapy is considered first-line therapy, particularly in pregnant patients.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Clonazepam therapy is incorrect. Clonazepam is a benzodiazepine that is useful in refractory anxiety disorders. However, cognitive behavioral therapy is considered first- line therapy, particularly in pregnant patients.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Neurologic evaluation is incorrect. This patient's numbness and paresthesia cannot be localized to a specific central nervous system region and are likely resulting from her anxiety.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Reexamination in 1 month is incorrect. This patient is experiencing impairing anxiety and requires immediate treatment.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presents with a handful of depressive symptoms (eg, appetite loss, concentration impairment, episodic decreased mood) after a stressor, which is consistent with adjustment disorder with depressed mood. Adjustment disorder presents with anxiety, depressive, and/or behavioral symptoms that do not fully meet anxiety or depressive disorder criteria.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Adjustment disorders involve emotional or behavioral symptoms, such as anxiety or depression, that arise in response to a stressor but do not meet full criteria for an anxiety or depressive disorder. This patient's behavior over the past month after a significant relationship stressor is most consistent with adjustment disorder with depressed mood.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of an emotional disturbance within 3 months of an identifiable stressor (eg, significant relationship stressors). The emotional disturbance manifests as marked distress out of proportion to the stressor and a significant impairment in daily functioning. Symptoms do not meet the criteria for other disorders and typically resolve within 6 months of the stressor ending or patient adaptation to the stressor.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Environmental modification to remove the stressor, when possible, can prevent adjustment disorder.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> As adjustment disorder is typically self-limited, the treatment of adjustment disorder commonly includes brief supportive psychotherapy, though brief pharmacotherapy that targets associated symptoms may be empirically provided.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Adjustment disorder should be differentiated from major depressive disorder, which can present with five or more depressive symptoms after a stressor. By definition, adjustment disorder with depressed mood presents with fewer than five depressive symptoms (eg, low mood, tearfulness, or feelings of hopelessness).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Brief psychotic disorder is incorrect. Brief psychotic disorder is characterized by at least one acute psychotic symptom (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior) lasting less than 1 month. This patient does not demonstrate psychotic symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Cyclothymic disorder is incorrect. Cyclothymic disorder refers to alternating hypomanic (eg, euphoric or irritable mood, increased activity level, decreased need for sleep) and depressive symptoms that do not meet full criteria for major depressive or manic episodes. This patient does not possess a history significant for manic symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Major depressive episode with atypical features is incorrect. Major depressive disorder involves 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor retardation, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (ie, decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). Major depressive disorder with atypical features involves hypersomnia, hyperphagia, mood reactivity, rejection sensitivity, and leaden paralysis. This patient does not meet the symptom number criteria for major depressive disorder.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is incorrect. Persistent depressive disorder refers to at least three depression symptoms that have endured for at least 2 years. This patient does not meet duration criteria for persistent depressive disorder.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, a teenager maintaining relationships with friends and family, and with consistent academic performance, is demonstrating normal development. Late adolescence often involves increased independence, boundary testing, staying out late, prioritizing peers over family, and experimenting with substances.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Individuals in their late teenage years will normally seek independence and may try alcohol, tobacco, or other substances. In the absence of problems with functioning at school and/or work, symptoms of a new mood disorder, engaging in violence or other illegal activity, or evidence of substance use disorder, this patient's presentation is typical of normal development.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Shifts in the behavior of teenagers may occur over time. Normal adolescent behavior involves seeking independence and forming identity, often through boundary testing and social exploration. Common changes include spending more time with peers, arguing more, staying out later, and experimenting with substances.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no prevention for a normally developing teenager. Prevention of substance use disorder may include treating psychiatric comorbidities, fostering strong social and community connections, and employing evidence-based interventions for preventing substance use disorders.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> There is no treatment for normal adolescent development. Treatment for substance use disorder may include inpatient or outpatient substance treatment programs, cognitive behavioral or other psychotherapy, or medication assisted treatment as appropriate.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The patient is staying out late, predominantly spending time with his friends, and experimenting with drug and alcohol use, which are all features of a normally developing teenager. Substance use disorder should be considered but is less likely in the absence of decline in his functioning. The patient has no symptoms of a mood disorder.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Alcohol use disorder is incorrect. There is no evidence in this patient of a persistent pattern of alcohol use or clear problems related to the use of alcohol. Alcohol use must be impairing the patient's functioning to qualify as a disorder.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Cannabis use disorder is incorrect. The few times this patient has tried cannabis with no clear adverse effects does not meet the criteria for cannabis use disorder. Cannabis use must interfere with functioning in life to qualify as a disorder.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Oppositional defiant disorder is incorrect. Oppositional defiant disorder manifests as a persistent pattern of angry mood, defiant behavior, and vindictiveness that causes distress and impaired functioning in the child or their caretakers. This patient's boundary testing behavior is not borne out of the need to rebel against authority but is typical of normal adolescent development.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Parent-child relational problem is not the most likely explanation for this patient's findings. This patient's behavior is not secondary to a poor relationship with his parents and is typical of normal adolescent development.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is incorrect. Persistent depressive disorder requires at least 2 years of depressed mood without any periods greater than 2 months of symptom resolution. There is no evidence of a persistently low mood in this patient, and his behaviors are normal for his age.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has schizophrenia. Schizophrenia is characterized by at least 6 months exhibiting two of the following five symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with schizophrenia demonstrate at least two of the following five symptoms: delusions (eg, this patient's fixation on solving an equation), hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms (eg, flat or blunted affect, apathy, alogia). Delusions may center around pre-existing themes in a patient's life. Many patients also illustrate prodromal periods of strange behavior and decreased functioning. The total duration of symptoms must exceed 6 months to meet diagnostic criteria.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> The cause of schizophrenia is thought to include dopamine dysregulation, and brain imaging typically shows decreased cortical volume and enlarged ventricles. A family history of bipolar disorder or schizophrenia is common in patients who are diagnosed.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Though there is no universal way to prevent schizophrenia, avoiding cannabis use during adolescence (especially in patients with a family history of serious mental illness) is likely beneficial for decreasing future schizophrenia risk.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of schizophrenia requires antipsychotics, which can be divided into two groups: typical (eg, haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine) and atypical (eg, quetiapine, aripiprazole, olanzapine).</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In obsessive-compulsive disorder, patients experience recurrent, intrusive, distressing thoughts (ie, obsessions) and perform repetitive mental or physical actions (ie, compulsions) to relieve the anxiety related to their obsessions. However, schizophrenic delusions are typically more rigid, and patient affect is more likely to be anxious than blunted as negative symptoms are uncommon in obsessive-compulsive disorder.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Avoidant personality disorder is incorrect. Avoidant personality disorder is a cluster C (anxious or fearful) personality disorder that features the avoidance of interpersonal contact due to feelings of social inadequacy and hypersensitivity to rejection. This patient's social avoidance is related to delusions rather than fear of rejection.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Bipolar disorder is incorrect. Bipolar disorder consists of episodes of mania, frequently in addition to hypomanic and depressive episodes. This patient does not demonstrate prominent mood symptoms (eg, elevated or depressed mood, sleep changes) and his illness course is constant rather than episodic. This patient most likely has schizophrenia.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Delusional disorder is incorrect. Delusional disorder is characterized by the presence of one or more delusions for 1 month or longer without other psychotic symptoms. This patient demonstrates negative symptoms, and his delusions are impacting his social functioning, making schizophrenia the most likely diagnosis.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Obsessive-compulsive disorder is incorrect. In obsessive-compulsive disorder, patients experience obsessions and perform compulsions to relieve the anxiety caused by their obsessions. However, delusions are typically more rigid, and the patient's affect is more likely to be anxious than blunted as negative symptoms are uncommon in obsessive- compulsive disorder. This patient demonstrates negative symptoms, and his delusions are impacting his social functioning, making schizophrenia the most likely diagnosis.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most appropriate next step is to discontinue quetiapine therapy and monitor this patient for behavioral changes. Antipsychotic medications, such as quetiapine, are associated with a black box warning of increased all-cause mortality in older patients with dementia and should be discontinued when no longer clinically indicated.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Deprescribing unnecessary medication is often beneficial, especially when they cause adverse effects or in older patients with multiple comorbidities and polypharmacy, where medication interactions are a concern. Antipsychotic medications carry a black box warning indicating an increased risk for all-cause mortality in older patients with dementia. This increase in mortality is likely due to cardiac events (eg, heart failure) and infectious causes (eg, pneumonia).</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> When a patient is hospitalized and develops hyperactive delirium, antipsychotic medications are commonly added to assist with agitation. If agitation secondary to delirium has improved by discharge, physicians should discontinue the antipsychotic medication prior to discharge while monitoring for worsening agitation. However, sometimes medications may inadvertently remain on a patient's medication list after discharge, or the patient may still be experiencing residual agitation at discharge. In this case, outpatient physicians should attempt to discontinue the antipsychotic medication as soon as it is no longer needed.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Some patients with dementia may have agitation associated with dementia. In refractory cases that do not respond to other medications for agitation, the benefits of an antipsychotic medication (eg, maintaining patient and family safety) may outweigh the risks.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choices A, D, and F:</b> Continue quetiapine therapy and reassure the patient's son that this drug's benefits outweigh its risks, divide the current dose of quetiapine to morning and evening administration, and switch from quetiapine to risperidone therapy are incorrect. This patient's agitation was secondary to delirium. Delirium and agitation typically improve with the treatment of the underlying condition, so long-term medications for agitation are typically unnecessary. Also, a morning dose of quetiapine is likely to increase daytime somnolence in this patient.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Continue quetiapine therapy for 4 months and then taper the dose is incorrect. This patient's agitation was secondary to delirium. Delirium and agitation typically improve with the treatment of the underlying condition, so long-term medications for agitation are typically unnecessary. If patients have residual agitation after discharge, quetiapine may be temporarily continued. However, this patient's agitation was improved.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Switch from quetiapine to diazepam therapy is not the most appropriate next step in management. Delirium and agitation typically improve with the treatment of the underlying condition, so long-term medications for agitation are typically unnecessary. Also, diazepam is on the American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria of medications that identifies potentially inappropriate medications for older patients due to its risk for cognitive impairment, delirium, falls, motor vehicle collisions, and fractures.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's presentation is inconsistent with a known medical condition and is suggestive of symptom fabrication (eg, artificially increasing her oral temperature and procuring a different person's urine for testing). In factitious disorder, patients consciously produce symptoms for primary gain.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> In factitious disorder, patients consciously produce symptoms (eg, purposely injure themselves, fake a fever by eating something warm, providing someone else's urine for laboratory study) for primary gain. Diagnosis is supported by inconsistencies in medical history or physical examination and a history of extensive clinical evaluations. If patients are directly confronted about their deceptive behaviors, it is common for them to discharge themselves from care.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Primary gain is the motivation to be cared for, which constitutes an unconscious motivator for the patient's conscious production of symptoms. Early losses, neglect, or abuse may disrupt patients' identity and relationships, and adopting the sick role may represent patients' attempts to cope with stress and stabilize their identity and relationships. Psychiatric comorbidities (eg, depression, substance use disorders, personality disorders) are common but not present in all patients.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause of factitious disorder is unknown, there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> To exclude a true fever and assist in establishing the factitious disorder diagnosis, a rectal temperature may be obtained in this patient. Treatment includes regular follow-up with one physician who oversees management (as opposed to symptom-triggered appointments) and possible psychotherapy to address underlying coping difficulties that are driving the patient's care-seeking behaviors.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In patients with symptoms without clear structural causes but with no evidence of deceptive behaviors, somatic symptom disorder or functional disorders should be considered. Malingering should be considered in patients who produce symptoms with a clear tangible gain (eg, disability benefits, avoiding prison time), which is known as secondary gain.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Conversion disorder is incorrect. Functional neurological symptom disorder (conversion disorder) involves neurologic symptoms, such as sensory or motor dysfunction, that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging. This patient does not present with neurologic dysfunction. Additionally, volitional symptom production is inconsistent with functional neurological symptom disorder.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Iliness anxiety disorder (hypochondriasis) is incorrect. Patients with illness anxiety disorder demonstrate persistently excessive anxiety and preoccupation about the possibility that they may have or may acquire a serious illness such that they perform frequent health-related behaviors or exhibit maladaptive avoidance. This patient does not fear that she has a serious illness.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Malingering is incorrect. Malingering is defined by falsification of symptoms to obtain a clear tangible gain (referred to as secondary gain). There is no clear tangible gain for this patient.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Somatic symptom disorder is incorrect. Patients with somatic symptom disorder are preoccupied with a somatic symptom or multiple symptoms such that these symptoms disrupt the patient's daily life. However, patients with somatic symptom disorder do not volitionally produce their symptoms. In this case, there is evidence for symptom falsification.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presents with pervasive social inhibition and rejection fears across contexts, which is most consistent with avoidant personality disorder. It is most appropriate to obtain specific additional history regarding this patient's reluctance to engage in new activities because they may prove embarrassing in order to confirm the diagnosis.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Avoidant personality disorder features the avoidance of interpersonal contact due to feelings of social inadequacy and hypersensitivity to rejection. These individuals, may present as extremely shy or timid, as seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Personality disorders are persistently maladaptive ways of relating to the self and to society that typically appear by early adulthood and are associated with significant social and occupational dysfunction. Avoidant personality disorder is a cluster C (anxious or fearful) personality disorder. It presents as pervasive social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy, and hypersensitivity to negative evaluation (eg, rejection).</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause is unknown, there is no universal way to prevent avoidant personality disorder.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Cognitive behavioral therapy may help address avoidance behaviors.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Social anxiety disorder presents similarly, but its behaviors may involve specific social situations as opposed to the pervasive inhibition characteristic of avoidant personality disorder.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Difficulty making decisions without advice and reassurance from others is incorrect. These features are characteristic of dependent personality disorder, a cluster C personality disorder. This patient presents as timid but prefers avoiding people.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Preoccupation with rules, lists, or organization is incorrect. This feature is consistent with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, a cluster C personality disorder. This patient's perfectionism does not extend beyond social settings.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Strange beliefs that influence behavior is incorrect. This feature is characteristic of schizotypal personality disorder, a cluster A (odd or eccentric) personality disorder manifesting with odd behavior and magical thinking and a constricted affect. Though this patient has a constricted affect because of social inhibition, she does not present with magical thinking or odd behavior.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Tendency to appear indifferent to praise or criticism from others is incorrect. This feature is consistent with schizoid personality disorder, a cluster A personality disorder characterized by extreme social detachment and a cold, restricted affect. Patients with schizoid or avoidant personality disorders may prefer to work the night shift to avoid interaction with others, but patients with avoidant personality disorder would be more likely to do so out of fear of social rejection, as seen in this patient.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Barbiturate overdose presents with somnolence, obtundation, or coma, plus cardiorespiratory depression. This patient, unconscious with bradycardia, hypotension, bradypnea, and sluggish pupils, most likely has overdosed on a barbiturate.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Diagnosis of barbiturate overdose is based on clinical history and physical examination, as toxicologic screening may be delayed or inaccurate.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Barbiturates increase the central nervous system activity of y-aminobutyric acid, the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter. Barbiturates are occasionally used as anticonvulsants in pediatric sedation and in the treatment of alcohol withdrawal, though their imprecise dosing, numerous medication interactions, and adverse effects of significant cardiorespiratory depression limit their use. They are still used nonmedically, including in suicide attempts.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Avoidance of use is the mainstay of prevention of barbiturate overdose.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> There is no specific or appropriate treatment for barbiturate overdose; supportive care to preserve life while permitting time for medication metabolism is the most appropriate management. Patients with significantly decreased mental status or respiratory depression may require intubation for airway protection and mechanical ventilation. Patients with cardiorespiratory depression may require intravenous fluid boluses or vasopressors. Cardiac pacing is rarely indicated but may be required in severe or refractory cases. Patients should also be tested and treated for co-ingestion, especially if overdose is in the context of attempted or suspected suicide.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's obtundation plus cardiorespiratory depression narrows the differential diagnosis to overdose of a sedative.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, and G.</p><p><b>Choice A and Choice G:</b> B-Adrenergic receptor antagonist and calcium-channel blocking agent are not most likely responsible for this patient's overdose. These medications can cause bradycardia and hypotension; however, they would not cause bradypnea/apnea and would not cause obtundation unless mental status was decreased due to severe hypotension, which is not the case with this patient.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Amantadine is incorrect. Amantadine overdose has several features that are primarily neurologic and anticholinergic. Neurologic symptoms can include tremor, ataxia, slurred speech, disorientation, confusion, or delirium, and, in severe cases, seizures or myoclonus. Hallucinations and visual disturbances are also seen. Anticholinergic symptoms include tachycardia, hypertension, warm, flushed, dry skin, visual disturbances (eg, mydriasis), urinary retention, abdominal discomfort, and altered mental status/agitation.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Amphetamine is incorrect. Amphetamines are stimulants that increase excitatory neurotransmitters. As such, elevated mood, loss of inhibition, hyperpyrexia, sympathetic excess, and increased thirst are common symptoms, along with tachycardia and hypertension. Severe intoxication can present with hyponatremia due to syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone along with increased consumption of water.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Anticholinergic is incorrect. Anticholinergic overdose symptoms include tachycardia, hypertension, warm, flushed, dry skin, visual disturbances, urinary retention, abdominal discomfort, and altered mental status/agitation.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Antihistamine is incorrect. Antihistamine intoxication presents with sedation/somnolence, though hemodynamic instability and apnea are not common features in isolated ingestion. Many antihistamines are also anticholinergic, which could also result in signs and symptoms such as tachycardia, hypertension, warm, flushed, dry skin, visual disturhances, urinary retention, abdominal discomfort, and altered mental status/agitation.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is sleep terror disorder, which is characterized by episodes of sympathetic hyperactivity and vocalizations. Management involves reassurance.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Common in childhood, sleep terror disorder is characterized by episodes of sympathetic hyperactivity (eg, tachycardia, tachypnea, diaphoresis, mydriasis) and vocalizations (eg, screams) during non-REM (stage N3) sleep. Episodes typically last minutes and occur during the first third of the night.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> During episodes, patients are typically partially awake and in a dissociative state, with features of sleep and wakefulness co-occurring on EEG and clinical assessment. Patients are commonly amnestic to the episode.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause is unknown, there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management involves reassurance, creating a safe sleep environment, and supporting sleep hygiene. Many children outgrow sleep terror disorder by adolescence.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In nightmare disorder, patients typically recall their episodes and experience nightmares during REM sleep (in 90-minute intervals throughout the night).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Benign rolandic epilepsy is incorrect. Benign rolandic epilepsy typically presents in childhood with unilateral sensorimotor symptoms of the face including drooling, twitching, and numbness. Sympathetic hyperarousal and vocalizations are atypical. Also, this patient does not present with focal facial sensorimotor symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Nightmare disorder is incorrect. Nightmare disorder is a common parasomnia involving recurrent frightening or extremely dysphoric dreams leading to distress and disrupted sleep. In nightmare disorder, patients typically recall their episodes and experience nightmares during REM sleep (in 90-minute intervals throughout the night). This patient is amnestic to her episodes, and episode timing is more consistent with non-REM sleep.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> REM sleep behavior disorder is incorrect. REM sleep behavior disorder features a lack of normal muscle atonia during REM sleep, most commonly occurring in older male patients. Patients typically act out their dreams in a coordinated though sometimes violent manner. This patient experiences episodes during non-REM sleep and does not act out her dreams.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Normal development is incorrect. This patient's symptoms are consistent with sleep terror disorder, which is considered a parasomnia.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, presenting with nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, altered mental status, and disheveled appearance, is likely to have abnormal liver function tests and hyperammonemia caused by his valproic acid medication.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Valproic acid toxicity should be suspected in patients taking valproic acid who disclose signs of hyperammonemia (eg, psychomotor slowing, altered mental status, flapping hand tremor) and gastrointestinal symptoms (eg, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea). The diagnosis can be confirmed through measurement of serum valproic acid and ammonia concentrations, and liver function testing.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Valproic acid is an anticonvulsant and mood stabilizer used in the management of seizure disorders, bipolar disorder, and schizoaffective disorder. Valproic acid can cause adverse effects, such as gastrointestinal distress, sedation, tremor, weight gain, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and hepatic effects, that range from benign increases in transaminases to hepatotoxicity with hyperammonemia (as seen in this patient). Weight, complete blood count, and liver function should be monitored regularly in patients taking valproic acid.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Regular assessment for signs and symptoms of valproic acid toxicity along with monitoring of liver function, serum ammonia concentration, and complete blood counts can permit early diagnosis of toxicity along with discontinuation.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Discontinuation of valproic acid in the setting of toxicity is appropriate to allow clearance of the medication and its metabolites. Dosing should be resumed at decreased concentrations, or a switch to an alternative agent may be more appropriate once toxicity resolves.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The combination of gastrointestinal and neurologic symptoms in a patient taking valproic acid strongly suggests valproic acid toxicity, which is typically associated with abnormal liver function tests.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Leukocyte count is incorrect. Leukocyte count may be decreased in valproic acid toxicity; however, this patient's symptoms relate to gastrointestinal dysfunction and altered mental status, which implicates hepatic function more than lymphopenia consequently.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Serum creatinine concentration is incorrect. Serum creatinine concentration is generally not affected by valproic acid. Most medications are metabolized through hepatic or renal routes; thus, increased serum creatinine concentration can indicate risk for toxicity. However, this patient's gastrointestinal and neurologic symptoms suggest hepatic dysfunction and not renal dysfunction as a cause.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Serum glucose concentration is not the most likely laboratory study to be abnormal in this patient. Assessing glucose in a patient with altered mental status is a priority; however, hypoglycemia generally presents with abnormal vital signs such as tachycardia and with tremor or diaphoresis on examination. These findings are not seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Urine toxicology screening is not the most likely laboratory study to be abnormal in this patient. Urine toxicology screening does not often correlate with current intoxication, as urine toxicology is generally positive long after intoxication has resolved.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, recovering from an emotionally and physically traumatic event, is demonstrating symptoms of acute stress disorder, such as easy startling, mood changes, and intrusive thoughts.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Acute stress disorder presents with symptoms of intrusive thoughts, hyperarousal, avoidance, and changes in mood and/or thinking beginning within 3 days and lasting up to 1 month after a traumatic event.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> The inciting traumatic event is defined as involving actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violation. Intrusive memories may manifest as daytime flashbacks, nightmares, or dissociative experiences. Changes in arousal and reactivity may show as hypervigilance, easy startle, anxiety, restlessness, and/or decreased concentration. Avoidance can present as refusal to discuss or think about the event.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> It is difficult to prevent acute stress disorder, as the incidence of traumatic events can be unpredictable.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Pharmacotherapy is typically not indicated, but supportive or cognitive behavioral therapy may be helpful.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Acute stress disorder occurs in the immediate short-term following a traumatic event, whereas post-traumatic stress disorder should be considered if symptoms begin or last over 1 month after the event.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Adjustment disorder is incorrect. Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor and may be associated with anxiety, depression, and/or conduct disturbances. Though this patient has anxiety and depressive symptoms, he also has several other symptoms (eg, hyperarousal, nightmares) that are more consistent with acute stress disorder.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Depersonalization-derealization disorder is incorrect. Depersonalization-derealization disorder is a dissociative disorder in which patients feel disconnected from their body and environment. Though the patient appears \"spaced out,\" it is not clear whether he is feeling disconnected from his body and environment, and he has several other symptoms more consistent with acute stress disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Major depression is incorrect. Major depressive disorder includes 2 or more weeks of five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor retardation, suicidal ideation, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (ie, decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). Though this patient has a depressed mood, he does not demonstrate other major depressive disorder symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Post-traumatic stress disorder is incorrect. Post-traumatic stress disorder refers to symptoms of intrusive thoughts/memories of trauma, hyperarousal, avoidance of trauma reminders, and/or negative mood/thoughts resulting from a traumatic event. Symptoms last more than 1 month following the event or may have delayed expression, usually beginning within 3 months but may occur months or years later. This patient's symptoms began in the immediate short-term following a traumatic event and continue less than 1 month at the time of visit.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In a patient with alcohol use disorder, the presence of confusion and nystagmus suggests Wernicke encephalopathy, and intravenous vitamin B1 (thiamine) must be administered prior to dextrose to prevent worsening of neurologic dysfunction.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Wernicke encephalopathy presents with confusion, ataxia, and nystagmus in a patient with alcohol use disorder or other cause of thiamine deficiency. The diagnosis is clinical but can be supported by decreased serum thiamine concentrations.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Wernicke encephalopathy is due to a deficiency of thiamine that typically occurs in patients with alcohol use disorder, although it can occur in other states of malnourishment. While a treatable condition, administering dextrose before thiamine can worsen the deficiency, resulting in permanent symptoms in addition to confabulation (ie, Korsakoff syndrome).</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Abstinence from alcohol and maintenance of a balanced diet is the best prevention for Wernicke encephalopathy. Patients with alcohol use disorder may also be given thiamine supplementation to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Thiamine is the best treatment for Wernicke encephalopathy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient has severe hypoglycemia and should be administered intravenous 50% dextrose. However, thiamine must be administered prior to dextrose if the patient is hypoglycemic.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Oral folic acid is incorrect. Deficiency of folic acid may be seen in individuals with alcohol use disorder and presents with macrocytic, megaloblastic anemia. Folic acid deficiency would not explain this patient's symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Intramuscular vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin) is incorrect. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency presents with macrocytic, megaloblastic anemia, polyneuropathy, and cognitive difficulties. Cobalamin deficiency is more likely in individuals with pernicious anemia or who follow a strict vegan diet than those with alcohol use disorder.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics is incorrect. Antibiotics would be correct in the management of acute infection; however, this patient does not show signs of infection, such as fever or leukocytosis.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Intravenous 50% dextrose is incorrect. Dextrose given before thiamine in a patient with Wernicke encephalopathy can worsen neurologic symptoms. This patient needs intravenous dextrose; however, thiamine is the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presents with longstanding depressed mood, decreased self-esteem, and insomnia, which is consistent with persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia).</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Persistent depressive disorder (PDD) refers to at least three depression symptoms that have endured for at least 2 years. By definition, patients must report a depressed mood on more days than not.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Other symptoms of PDD include neurovegetative symptoms (ie, decreased energy, sleep disturbances, appetite disturbances), impaired concentration, low self-esteem, and hopelessness.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause is unknown, there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Emerging evidence indicates that PDD should be managed similarly to major depressive disorder (MDD). Antidepressant medication plus psychotherapy is most effective. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors are generally the first-line medications given their low adverse effect burden.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> PDD and MDD may present similarly. However, by definition, patients with PDD do not meet full criteria for MDD.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder is incorrect. Circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder is a common cause of insomnia characterized by a shifted or irregular sleep- wake cycle relative to a typical day-night cycle. This patient experiences insomnia but does not report a shifted sleep schedule. Also, he demonstrates additional mood symptoms consistent with PDD.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dependent personality disorder is incorrect. Dependent personality disorder is a cluster C (anxious or fearful) personality disorder in which the patient is excessively submissive. This patient presents with decreased self-esteem but does not demonstrate a pervasive pattern of deferring to others' needs.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Major depressive disorder is incorrect. Symptoms of MDD include 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia (eg, decreased interest in socializing), guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms. Though this patient demonstrates a depressed mood and sleep difficulties, he does not meet the symptom number criteria for MDD.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Somatic symptom disorder is incorrect. Patients with somatic symptom disorder are preoccupied with an existing somatic symptom such that these symptoms disrupt the patient's daily life. This patient presents with symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome but is not particularly preoccupied with these symptoms.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient is most likely using altruism to cope with her current situation. Altruism helps a person manage intolerable emotions through the gratification of helping others. key Concept: When any person experiences painful thoughts, realities, or feelings, such as anxiety or sadness, the mind has several unconscious methods of shielding the person from conscious awareness of the thought, reality, or affect. These methods are called defense mechanisms.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Altruism helps a person manage emotional conflict through helping others (eg, starting a free clinic). Altruistic acts may relieve painful emotions by providing vicarious gratification or by helping individuals rewrite a past situation in which they did not receive the help they needed. Altruism is considered an adaptive defense as it channels affects into socially helpful action.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Other adaptive defensive mechanisms include humor, sublimation, and suppression, among others.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Displacement is incorrect. In displacement, a person may transfer negative feelings from an unacceptable person to a more acceptable person. For instance, a woman cannot tolerate being angry at her spouse for cheating because of the fear she will need to divorce him, so her unconscious mind transfers the anger to her mother who she cannot divorce and is therefore a more acceptable target. This patient is not transferring negative feelings across people.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Intellectualization is incorrect. In intellectualization, the individual avoids painful emotions by overreliance on abstract thinking rather than emoting. Though this patient is appropriately focusing on her medical school examinations, it does not appear she is excessively using abstract thinking.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Projection is incorrect. Projection involves the patient attributing intolerable feelings to someone else. For example, a woman feels angry at her partner, but cannot tolerate feeling angry at her partner so perceives that her partner is actually angry with her. This patient does not present any evidence of projection.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Rationalization is incorrect. In rationalization, individuals devise a reassuring explanation for their or others\" actions. For example, the individual may avoid quilt related to a self-serving action by verbalizing a more socially acceptable motivation for an action (eg, \"I only ate your slice of cake so that you will not gain weight\"). This patient does not perform self-serving acts.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has schizophrenia, which is characterized by at least 6 months exhibiting two of the following five symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms. Atypical antipsychotic therapy (eg, quetiapine) is the first-line treatment.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with schizophrenia demonstrate at least two of the following five symptoms: delusions (eg, persecutory delusions), hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, or negative symptoms (eg, flat affect, apathy, alogia). Negative symptoms may also manifest as poor personal hygiene. Many patients also illustrate prodromal periods of strange behavior and decreased functioning. The total duration of symptoms must exceed 6 months to meet diagnostic criteria.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> The cause of schizophrenia is thought to include dopamine dysregulation, and brain imaging typically shows decreased cortical volume and enlarged ventricles. A family history of bipolar disorder or schizophrenia is common in patients who are diagnosed.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Though there is no universal way to prevent schizophrenia, avoiding cannabis use during adolescence (especially in patients with a family history of serious mental illness) is likely beneficial for decreasing future schizophrenia risk.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of schizophrenia requires antipsychotics, which can be divided into two groups: typical (eg, haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine) and atypical (eg, quetiapine, aripiprazole, olanzapine).</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Typical antipsychotics are effective. However, their potent dopamine receptor antagonism is associated with movement disorders, such as acute dystonia, parkinsonism, akathisia, and tardive dyskinesia. As atypical antipsychotics carry a decreased movement disorder risk, they are commonly considered to be first-line treatments.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Clozapine is incorrect. Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic medication used in treatment-refractory schizophrenia. Serious adverse effects, such as agranulocytosis, myocarditis, and seizures, prohibit the use of clozapine as an initial treatment for patients with schizophrenia.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Divalproex is incorrect. Divalproex is an anticonvulsant and mood stabilizer used in the treatment of bipolar disorder and schizoaffective disorder. As it addresses mood but not psychotic symptoms, it is not a useful pharmacotherapy for schizophrenia.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lithium carbonate is incorrect. Lithium carbonate is a mood stabilizer used in the treatment of bipolar disorder and schizoaffective disorder. Lithium carbonate addresses mood but not psychotic symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Lorazepam is incorrect. Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine medication used for acute anxiety, alcohol withdrawal, and seizures. Lorazepam does not address psychotic symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Phenelzine is incorrect. Phenelzine is a monoamine oxidase inhibitor used in the treatment of depression and anxiety disorders. Phenelzine does not address psychotic symptoms.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Panic disorder is characterized by recurrent, unexpected panic attacks that are associated with worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers. The most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient targets serotonin through the use of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs).</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> This patient's monthlong history of increasingly frequent episodes of anxiety accompanied by shortness of breath, sweating, finger tingling, and a rapid heartbeat is most suggestive of panic attacks. He most likely has panic disorder, which is characterized by recurrent panic attacks that are unexpected and associated with worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Panic attacks feature acute fear or anxiety that peak within minutes and are associated with four additional physical symptoms or associated mental states. These. additional symptoms may include heart palpitations or tachycardia, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, the sensation of choking, gastrointestinal distress, paresthesias, sweating, chils, trembling, derealization, the fear of dying, and/or the fear of losing control.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the cause of panic disorder is multifactorial (ie, involving various genetic and environmental triggers), there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment typically includes psychotherapy and long-term SSRI (eg, sertraline) therapy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Short-term benzodiazepines can help with anxiety while awaiting the effect of SSRIs, which typically takes weeks.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Acetylcholine is incorrect. Medications that increase acetylcholine signaling (eg, cholinesterase inhibitors, such as donepezil) are used in dementia and myasthenia gravis. Acetylcholine antagonists (eg, benztropine) lead to relief of drug-induced parkinsonism along with sedation, urinary retention, and constipation. These medications are not used to treat anxiety disorders.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dopamine is incorrect. Dopamine agonists (eg, pramipexole) are used for symptomatic treatment in Parkinson disease, while dopamine antagonists (eg, antipsychotic medications) are used to treat schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. While antipsychotics can be used for refractory anxiety, SSRIs have stronger evidence and more benign adverse effect profiles, and are, therefore, first-line treatments for anxiety disorders.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Glutamate is incorrect. Medications that antagonize glutamate (eg, memantine) are used in the treatment of dementia. These medications are not used to treat anxiety disorders.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Histamine is incorrect. Hydroxyzine is an antihistamine medication used off-label as an as-needed anxiolytic. Hydroxyzine can be useful as an adjunctive medication for panic attacks, but SSRIs are evidence-based first-line treatments.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has major depressive disorder (MDD), peripartum onset. MDD presents with depressed mood, decreased interest, feelings of guilt or worthlessness, decreased concentration, neurovegetative changes (ie, changes in sleep and appetite, decreased energy, and slowed movement), and/or thoughts of death or suicide. MDD symptoms make daily activities (eg, leaving the house) challenging.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> A diagnosis of MDD is made when a patient experiences five or more of the following symptoms over a 2-week period or greater: depressed mood, anhedonia, change in appetite or weight, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or retardation, fatigue, feelings of quilt or worthlessness, diminished ability to think or concentrate, and recurrent thoughts of death including suicidal ideation with or without a plan. Patients commonly experience dally dysfunction because of their depressive symptoms. This patient demonstrates several of these symptoms since the birth of her baby. MDD with peripartum onset occurs during pregnancy or within 4 weeks after delivery.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> MDD with peripartum onset can lead to adverse consequences including a decreased likelihood of breast-feeding, impaired bonding with the infant, marital discord, poorer infant health, infanticide, and suicide.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Increasing psychosocial support, exercising, and seeking counseling may help prevent MDD with peripartum onset.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management of MDD may involve psychotherapy (eg, cognitive behavioral therapy) and/or antidepressant therapy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Postpartum blues is a common, transient state that may be related to hormonal changes and resolves within 2 weeks of delivery. Patients with postpartum blues do not meet MDD criteria.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Postpartum blues is incorrect. Postpartum blues is a common, transient state of sadness, crying, irritability, and/or anxiety that may be related to hormonal changes and resolves within 2 weeks of delivery. Patients with postpartum blues do not meet MDD criteria.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Postpartum psychosis is incorrect. Postpartum psychosis refers to the postpartum onset of psychotic symptoms (eg, delusions, hallucinations). Though this patient has anxiety about being a good mother, she does not demonstrate a fixed, false belief that would constitute a delusion.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Postpartum thyroiditis is incorrect. Though postpartum thyroiditis can present with anxiety, it also commonly presents with other somatic symptoms, such as heart palpitations and physical examination findings (eg, dry skin), along with laboratory study findings consistent with thyroid dysfunction. '</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Normal postpartum course is incorrect. This patient demonstrates symptoms consistent with MDD. which is not typical in the normal postpartum course.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient pervasively perceives that others are persecuting him, which is behavior consistent with paranoid personality disorder. Paranoid personality disorder presents with pervasive mistrust of others and viewing others as possessing malicious intent.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with paranoid personality disorder view others as malevolent (such as this patient's distrust of his coworkers) and often question and distrust their motives as a result. These individuals may present as angry and reluctant to rely on others.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Personality disorders are persistently maladaptive ways of relating to the self and to society that typically appear by early adulthood. Personality disorders are associated with significant social and occupational dysfunction. Paranoid personality disorder is a cluster A (odd or eccentric) personality disorder.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause is unknown, there is no universal way to prevent paranoid personality disorder.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Patients with paranoid personality disorder may be hesitant to engage in treatment, though some patients may benefit from psychotherapy targeting their paranoid 'ideation.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Schizotypal personality disorder along with psychotic disorders, such as schizophrenia and delusional disorder, should be excluded for this patient. While all can present with paranoia, schizotypal personality disorder and schizophrenia both typically present with perceptual disturbances (eg, hallucinations), which are not seen in this patient. Furthermore, patients with delusional disorder may have one or a few specific false beliefs, while patients with paranoid personality disorder are pervasively paranoid across all contexts.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Antisocial personality disorder is incorrect. Antisocial personality disorder is a cluster B (emotional or dramatic) personality disorder that arises from a deficit in empathy, resulting in pervasive violations of others' rights, aggression, and a hostile and manipulative attitude toward others. Though this patient experiences anger and hostility toward others, he does not violate others' rights.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Narcissistic personality disorder is incorrect. Narcissistic personality disorder is a cluster B personality disorder that is characterized by fragile self-esteem and compensatory arrogant, self-aggrandizing behavior to gain admiration, sometimes at others' expense. This patient's main issue is that he misinterprets others' intentions as being malicious toward him or unfairly criticizing him. His behavior is defensive rather than self-aggrandizing.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is incorrect. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, a cluster C (anxious or fearful) personality disorder, features preoccupation with rules, lists, or organization. Though this patient speaks of his work, he is mainly preoccupied with perceived slights from others, consistent with paranoid personality disorder.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Schizotypal personality disorder is incorrect. Schizotypal personality disorder is a cluster A personality disorder manifesting with odd behavior and magical thinking and a constricted affect. This patient does not demonstrate magical thinking or odd behavior.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The presence of focal neurologic deficits suggest a structural neurologic cause of this patient's depressive and cognitive symptoms and should prompt an MRI of the brain. This patient has chronic hepatitis as a malignancy risk factor, so primary brain cancer should be considered in the differential diagnosis.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Patients who present with the combination of neuropsychiatric symptoms (eg, depression, psychosis, cognitive impairment) and focal neurologic deficits require brain imaging. Focal neurologic deficits commonly indicate that a lesion of a particular central nervous system area (eg, cancer, stroke, abscess) is present. MRI of the brain is the most sensitive imaging modality for detecting structural lesions. Older age onset of psychiatric symptoms without a clear stressor or trigger is supportive of a secondary cause rather than a primary psychiatric disorder.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Patients with primary brain cancer have highly variable clinical presentations depending on the tumor size and location. The most common symptoms are headache, focal neurologic deficits, seizures, and neuropsychiatric symptoms.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Other localized lesions, such as stroke, hematoma, and abscess, should be considered. Major depressive disorder is less likely given the focal neurologic deficits and gross cognitive impairment.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, and G.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Neuropsychological testing is incorrect. Neuropsychological testing can be used when the cause of a patient's cognitive impairment is unclear. However, the presence of focal neurologic deficits suggest a structural lesion.</p><p><b>Choices B, C, and D:</b> Measurement of serum ammonia concentration, measurement of serum urea nitrogen concentration, and thyroid function tests are incorrect. Though abnormalities in these laboratory studies can present with cognitive impairment and associated psychiatric symptoms, focal neurologic deficits would be atypical. Thyroid dysfunction would also present with other physical examination signs, such as hair and skin changes.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> EEG is incorrect. EEG helps to assess seizure activity as the cause of cognitive or psychiatric symptoms. This patient does not show episodic motor signs consistent with seizures. Though subclinical seizures can cause cognitive impairment without visible seizure activity, MRI of the brain should be pursued first, as the presence of focal neurologic deficits suggest a structural lesion.</p><p><b>Choice G:</b> Lumbar puncture is incorrect. Lumbar puncture may be pursued to exclude central nervous system infections or autoimmune encephalitis as the cause of neuropsychiatric symptoms. However, MRI of the brain is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis, as the presence of focal neurologic deficits suggest a structural lesion.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient is likely experiencing acute mania with psychosis. Given that symptom timing correlated with duloxetine use, this patient most likely has medication-induced mood disorder.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Antidepressant initiation or dose escalation can lead to medication-induced mania. Acute mania typically manifests with an irritable or euphoric (and at times labile) mood, increased energy, a decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsibility, and/or grandiose thinking. In severe cases, patients can experience psychosis, as in this case where the patient appears internally stimulated (eg, late-night baking while talking to herself).</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Antidepressant-induced mania is somewhat common in patients with underlying bipolar disorder and less common, but possible, in patients with unipolar major depressive disorder. Tricyclic antidepressants carry the highest risk, followed by serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Carefully screening for bipolar disorder prior to initiating antidepressants can help mitigate the risk for medication-induced mania.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management typically involves antidepressant discontinuation and mood stabilizer initiation.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Underiying bipolar disorder should be considered in patients who have experienced antidepressant-induced mania,</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Major depressive disorder with psychotic features is incorrect. Major depressive disorder with psychotic features includes 2 or more weeks of depressive symptoms (eg, depressive symptoms, neurovegetative symptoms) and psychotic symptoms that occur concurrently. This patient demonstrates a delusional belief secondary to manic symptoms but does not currently demonstrate depressive symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Schizoaffective disorder is incorrect. Schizoaffective disorder is characterized by psychotic symptoms (eg, delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, and/or negative symptoms) along with significant mood episodes (eg, major depressive, manic episodes). Patients must demonstrate isolated psychotic symptoms without mood symptoms for at least 2 weeks. This patient demonstrates manic symptoms in conjunction with psychotic symptoms, indicating that her psychotic symptoms are likely secondary to her mood disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Schizophrenia is incorrect. Schizophrenia disorder involves 6 months or longer of two of the following: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or disorganized behavior (known as positive symptoms), or negative symptoms (eq, flat affect, apathy, alogia). This patient demonstrates manic symptoms in conjunction with psychotic symptoms, indicating that her psychotic symptoms are likely secondary to her mood disorder.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Serotonin syndrome is incorrect. Serotonin syndrome, the result of serotonin toxicity, can lead to the triad of acute confusion, autonomic hyperactivity (ie, hyperthermia, tachycardia, hypertension), and neuromuscular excitability (ie, tremor, myoclonus, hyperreflexia). This patient is hemodynamically stable and has a normal physical examination.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient is taking lithium carbonate, which has a narrow therapeutic index, so recommending staying well-hydrated in hot weather is the most appropriate recommendation to prevent adverse effects.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Lithium carbonate is used as a mood stabilizer in bipolar disorder. However, it is cationic and renally cleared, and dehydration or imbalances in electrolytes can result in toxicity, which can be severe and cause permanent neurologic deficits if not promptly diagnosed and managed. Lithium carbonate crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta, and is excreted in breast milk.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> At therapeutic doses, lithium carbonate can cause adverse effects, such as tremor, nystagmus, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, and thyroid dysfunction. Risk factors for lithium toxicity include intentional or inadvertent overuse, dehydration (including that resulting from diabetes insipidus), or any factor impairing renal clearance (eg, inadequate fluid intake during hot weather). Additionally, lithium carbonate is freely filtered by the kidneys and reabsorbed in states of dehydration or diuretic use, which can increase serum concentration and toxicity risk. In severe toxicity, patients may experience seizures, arrhythmias, and coma. Some neurologic manifestations may be irreversible 'in severe cases.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Recognizing lithium carbonate's adverse effects and toxicity risk factors highlights the importance of staying well-hydrated in hot weather to prevent increased serum concentrations.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Avoiding alcoholic beverages is not the most appropriate recommendation. While alcoholic beverages can cause dehydration through diuresis, this patient only drinks one to two alcoholic beverages monthly, which is not a significant amount and unlikely to influence his medications or hydration status in any meaningful manner.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Avoiding international travel is incorrect. A patient with stable health status on stable long-term dosing of a medication does not need to be on any specific travel restriction.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Eating a diet high in complex carbohydrates and fiber is incorrect. While eating such a diet may be appropriate depending on the patient's BMI and comorbidities and will likely be an excellent choice in disease prevention and management if he is otherwise healthy, such a diet will neither cause nor prevent lithium toxicity.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Exercising regularly is incorrect. Exercising regularly is also an excellent preventive healthcare measure. Excessive exercise or exercise during hot weather could increase his risk for dehydration, so he should also be counseled to remain well-hydrated in these scenarios. However, exercising regularly will neither cause nor prevent lithium toxicity.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, who presents with the subacute onset of memory difficulty along with several depressive symptoms (eg, affect constriction, feelings of worthlessness, decreased interest in activities, decreased energy, decreased concentration), is likely experiencing major depressive disorder (MDD).</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Symptoms of MDD include 2 or more weeks of depressed mood, anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms (ie, decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance). Older patients may experience depression-related cognitive dysfunction (previously known as pseudodementia), which most commonly manifests as decreased processing speed, impaired attention, executive dysfunction, and memory impairment. Decreased effort on Cognitive testing is also supportive of this diagnosis.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Older patients may view their neurovegetative symptoms as a manifestation of normal aging or a comorbid medical condition rather than depression. Consequently, older patients who are depressed may struggle to express their depressed mood. MDD should be suspected in this patient with many other depressive symptoms.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause of MDD is unknown, there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Mood and cognition typically improve with antidepressant medication and/or psychotherapy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Acute and fluctuating cognitive changes are consistent with delirium. Chronic, progressive cognitive changes (particularly when mood symptoms are absent) are more consistent with dementia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Delirium is incorrect. Delirium is an acute confusional state commonly associated with acute medical illness in older patients. Delirium presents with acute disturbances in awareness, attention, and baseline cognition that fluctuate in severity over the course of the day. This patient instead presents with subacute, nonfluctuating cognitive symptoms and does not present with a clear precipitating medical cause of delirium.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dementia, Alzheimer type, is incorrect. Dementia, Alzheimer type, causes chronic, progressive impairments in language, memory, perception, motor skills, attention, orientation, problem-solving, and executive function. A subacute course that involves associated depressive symptoms and decreased effort on cognitive examination is more consistent with MDD.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Dissociative amnesia is incorrect. Dissociative amnesia refers to the inability to recall traumatic or anxiety-inducing events secondary to psychological distress. This patient instead has more generalized cognitive deficits, presenting with inattention, memory difficulty, and decreased effort on cognitive testing.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Normal aging is incorrect. Though normal aging is associated with mild deficits in processing speed, attention, memory, and executive function, these deficits do not typically cause daily dysfunction, as seen in this patient. Also, this patient presents with depressive symptoms, which are not consistent with normal aging but with MDD.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Metoclopramide, a dopamine antagonist, would place this patient at increased risk for recurrent neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS).</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> NMS is a life-threatening condition associated with the antagonism of dopamine that presents with altered mental status, muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic dysfunction (eg, tachycardia, sweating).</p><p><b>Definition:</b> NMS is commonly caused by antipsychotic medications (especially high-potency antipsychotic medications) and antidopaminergic nausea medications (eg, metoclopramide). Rigidity results from dopamine antagonism in the nigrostriatal pathway, while autonomic dysfunction and hyperthermia may result from dopamine antagonism in the hypothalamus. Characteristic laboratory abnormalities include leukocytosis and increased serum creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and alkaline phosphatase (due to prolonged muscle rigidity and consequent myocyte damage). Electrolyte abnormalities (eg, hypo- or hypernatremia, hypocalcemia) may also be shown.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Avoidance of antidopaminergic medications is the only specific prevention, though such avoidance is not always feasible especially if the benefits of an antidopaminergic medication outweigh the risks. Patients for whom therapy with antidopaminergic agents has failed due to NMS should be switched to alternative therapeutic agents to prevent recurrence.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment is supportive and includes discontinuation of the offending medication, hydration, cooling blankets, and benzodiazepines for agitation if needed.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's acute hyperthermia, altered mental status, muscle rigidity, and autonomic instability are consistent with NMS. Understanding that NMS is caused by antidopaminergic agents narrows the differential of safe pharmacologic agents to those other than metoclopramide or other dopamine antagonists.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Bromocriptine is incorrect. Bromocriptine, a dopamine D2 receptor agonist, is used in the management of hyperprolactinemia and acromegaly, among other indications. In NMS, dopamine agonists may help restore dopaminergic activity and counteract the dopamine receptor blockade caused by antipsychotic medications.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dantrolene is incorrect. Dantrolene is a skeletal muscle relaxer used in the management of malignant hyperthermia and refractory NMS. Its use is appropriate in this setting.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Diphenhydramine is incorrect. Diphenhydramine is an antihistamine and anticholinergic used in the management of allergies, anxiety, and insomnia. Diphenhydramine would be contraindicated in anticholinergic syndrome. This patient presents with NMS.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Lorazepam is incorrect. Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that increases y-aminobutyric acid activity in the central nervous system. It may be used for sedation or to reduce muscle rigidity in the management of NMS, but it would not put this patient at increased risk for recurrence of his symptoms.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Counseling parents of a fetus with a new diagnosis of a genetic disorder can be challenging. The first step is investigating what they already know about the disorder by asking if they have any questions.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Down syndrome is a genetic disorder characterized by an array of morphologic features and intellectual disability. Effective communication after the diagnosis of Down syndrome is disclosed is important and initially should focus on what the parents already know to help them understand the condition and make knowledgeable decisions regarding steps moving forward.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> When breaking the news of a life-changing diagnosis to a patient or their family members, the physician should first ask open-ended questions to maintain therapeutic alliance and understand how to best provide information to the patient or their family. This information will provide the physician an opportunity to clarify misconceptions and ensure that the parents have information to make decisions moving forward.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Depending on patient preference and state legal status, a number of options are available for them to consider; however, the most important initial step is to elicit what they already know about Down syndrome.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> \"I am sorry that this is so difficult, but there are a number of support groups available for parents of children with Down syndrome\" is incorrect. This response does not help the parents understand the diagnosis and assumes they will continue with the pregnancy.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> \"Several options are available for you to consider, including adoption or termination of the pregnancy\" is incorrect. While options available to the parents will need to be explained, this response does not allow for an initial attempt to understand or clarify the parents' understanding of Down syndrome, so they can make a knowledgeable decision regarding steps moving forward.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> \"Since this is devastating news, let's sit quietly for a moment to think about your concerns\" is not the most appropriate response. It may be helpful to allow time for the parents to absorb new information and think about questions; however, it is not appropriate to label the diagnosis of Down syndrome as devastating, and this option avoids gathering information to best advise the parents.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> \"While raising a child with Down syndrome will present some challenges, you should be assured your child can live a healthy life\" is not the most appropriate response. Itis true that people with Down syndrome can live healthy lives. However, this statement does not elicit the parents' understanding of Down syndrome and it assumes they will proceed with the pregnancy.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's presentation, which includes rebellious and exploratory behavior, substance use that is not yet impairing her academic or familial life, and attitude change represent normal teenage behavior and exploration. She and her family should be counseled about the risks of substance use, as while she is maintaining her grades and family life for now, progressive or increasing substance use may eventually impair one or more aspects of her life.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> During the teenage years, patients may begin to rebel against parental or scholastic authority, explore and try things without complete consideration of consequences, and express their desires and wishes as they transition from childhood to adulthood. In this case, trying cannabis, cigarettes, and alcohol in limited fashion is generally normal exploration, though parents and physicians have a duty to guide and advise patients about safe choices to prevent negative consequences.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Cannabis is a hallucinogen that causes euphoria, anxiety, paranoia, perception of slowed time, impaired judgement, increased appetite, dry mouth, slow speech, prolonged response latency, lassitude, and inability to perform complex tasks, though severity of cannabis intoxication is based on the amount used and patient tolerance. Tachycardia, hypertension, and conjunctival injection may be seen on examination. This patient reports use of cigarettes daily, cannabis on occasion, and occasional alcohol use. Her grades, while slightly lower, are above average. She continues to care about herself. These features argue for a teenager trying and exploring aspects of life and learning her preferences/how her mind and body work. She likely cannot conceptualize or appreciate the consequences of excess use, as the prefrontal cortex required for such executive decision making is undeveloped in teenagers. As such, parents and physician have a duty to counsel and guide about safe choices, and to prevent negative consequences, such as traumatic accidents, addiction, failing grades, undesired pregnancy, and loss of relationships.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's limited cannabis use and absence of substantial life-affecting consequences indicates that counseling is all that is required currently. If her substance use were more severe or if it were affecting aspects of her life, substance use disorder treatment programs might then be necessary.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B and Choice C:</b> Recommend an inpatient substance abuse treatment program and recommend an outpatient substance abuse treatment program are not the most appropriate next steps in management. This patient's substance use is on an inconsistent basis and is not currently limiting or significantly affecting her life or the lives of others.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Begin nicotine patch therapy is incorrect. This patient has been smoking two cigarettes daily, which is likely not significant enough to merit nicotine replacement therapy. Furthermore, she states she is planning to stop using substances, which at her level of use, may be enough to maintain cessation. If she is unsuccessful or if she begins smoking more heavily and wishes to quit, nicotine replacement therapy could be considered.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Begin a trial of bupropion is not the most appropriate next step in management. Bupropion is used as an antidepressant and smoking cessation aid. This patient does not have clinical signs of major depressive disorder, and her smoking is at a minimal level.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: An acetaminophen concentration of 180 pg/mL at 4 hours postingestion is a hepatotoxic concentration and an indication for therapy with oral administration of -acetylcysteine. In this case, this patient likely attempted suicide via acetaminophen overdose.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Acetaminophen poisoning presents with nonspecific symptoms of fatigue, malaise, and abdominal pain followed by signs and symptoms of liver failure, including Jaundice, altered mental status, coagulopathy, and marked increases in serum transaminase concentrations. Acetaminophen poisoning should be suspected based on history of 'ingestion or in cases of potential suicide. A serum acetaminophen concentration at 4 hours postingestion should be obtained to assess for toxicity.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Most patients with acetaminophen toxicity are asymptomatic and have a normal physical examination during the first 24 hours following ingestion. They may not develop evidence of hepatotoxicity until 24 to 48 hours after ingestion. Acetaminophen toxicity is treated by repleting hepatic stores of glutathione through intravenous or oral administration of N-acetylcysteine. The likelihood of developing hepatotoxicity can be stratified by obtaining a serum acetaminophen concentration and plotting the result against the time from ingestion on the Revised Rumack-Matthew nomogram. Additionally, this patient's acute suicide attempt via acetaminophen ingestion requires psychiatric stabilization. He is at higher risk for suicide attempt and dying by suicide given his biological sex and history of substance use disorder.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Suicide attempts can be prevented with appropriate psychiatric care and therapy, and avoidance/treatment of modifiable risk factors. Modifiable risk factors include (but are not limited to) the presence of substance use disorder, depression or psychiatric comorbidities (eg, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia), a lack of social support or relationships, and the presence of medical illness. Removal of weapons or medications that may be used for self-harm is also a key preventive measure.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> N-acetylcysteine should be given to all patients at risk for hepatotoxicity as a result of excessive acetaminophen ingestion regardless of symptoms. Following management of acetaminophen overdose, patients should be evaluated and treated for suicidal ideation or attempt with psychiatric stabilization.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's history of acetaminophen overdose in addition to his current toxic serum acetaminophen concentration indicates the need for treatment to prevent hepatotoxicity. The most appropriate treatment is N-acetylcysteine.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Discharge from emergency department with psychiatric follow-up is incorrect. This patient has ingested a toxic amount of acetaminophen in a suicide attempt and requires medical and psychiatric stabilization.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> 24-Hour observation followed by repeat measurement of serum acetaminophen concentration is not the most appropriate next step in management. While this patient. will require observation and repeat serum acetaminophen concentration measurement, he also requires oral administration of N-acetylcysteine to prevent hepatotoxicity.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Intravenous administration of mannitol is incorrect. Intravenous administration of mannitol is appropriate in the management of increased intracranial pressure, such as in cases of cerebral edema. This patient is alert, oriented, and has no signs or symptoms of increased intracranial pressure or cerebral edema (eg, altered mental status, head trauma, or neurologic deficits).</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Intravenous administration of 0.9% saline is incorrect. Intravenous administration of 0.9% saline is appropriate in cases of toxic ingestion from overdoses, such as of Bradrenergic blockers, or in many cases of overdose with hypotension. This patient is normotensive and presents with an overdose with a specific treatment.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Admission to the psychiatric unit for evaluation of suicidal ideation is not the most appropriate next step in management. Admission to the psychiatric unit for evaluation of suicidal ideation is appropriate after, not before, medical stabilization of acetaminophen overdose.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hospitalization for medical detoxification is appropriate for patients with polysubstance use disorder for whom withdrawal could lead to life-threatening consequences. This patient's alcohol and benzodiazepine use could lead to seizures, autonomic instability, and delirium tremens in severe withdrawal.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal can occur within 6 to 12 hours of cessation of use. Seizures, autonomic instability, and delirium tremens can be life-threatening consequences. Diagnosis is based on clinical assessment.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Alcohol and benzodiazepines increase the central nervous system activity of y-aminobutyric acid (GABA), the main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Abrupt discontinuation of alcohol or benzodiazepines leads to sympathetic excess. Uncomplicated alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal typically presents with tremors, anxiety, restlessness, headache, nausea, and diaphoresis, which can occur hours after cessation. Delirium tremens, the most concerning and life-threatening complication, involves severe confusion and disorientation, fluctuations in consciousness, agitation, visual hallucinations, and autonomic instability (ie, fluctuations in heart rate and blood pressure with hyperthermia).</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Avoidance of substance use prevents progression to withdrawal. For patients with existing heavy alcohol or benzodiazepine use, hospitalization for medical detoxification with gradually decreased GABA replacement therapy should occur.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal are treated with replacement therapy, generally with benzodiazepines, such as lorazepam and diazepam, followed by a gradual taper over days to weeks. Fluid and electrolyte derangements should be corrected. Vitamin repletion, especially vitamin B1 (thiamine), folic acid, and magnesium, should 'occur in alcohol withdrawal. Concomitant withdrawal, likely opioid for this patient, should be managed with supportive care and/or agonist therapy with methadone or buprenorphine.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's alcohol and benzodiazepine use at daily and moderate-to-severe levels necessitates inpatient detoxification to prevent life-threatening withdrawal. Opioid withdrawal, while causing significant and distressing symptoms, is not life-threatening and does not otherwise require inpatient therapy unless patients are unable to maintain their fluid and nutritional status or manage pain in the outpatient setting.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A and Choice D:</b> Buprenorphine therapy and methadone maintenance therapy are not the most appropriate next steps in management. Buprenorphine or methadone therapy are appropriate for opioid use disorder, which this patient has; however, addressing opioid use would only address one aspect of her polysubstance use disorder. Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal are life-threatening and necessitate hospitalization for medical detoxification.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Intensive psychosocial treatment in a residential setting is not the best choice. Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal are life-threatening and necessitate hospitalization for medical detoxification. Psychosocial treatment may be beneficial to prevent relapses and provide social support, but it does not take the place of immediate management of alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Tapered doses of diazepam as an outpatient is incorrect. Tapered doses of diazepam as an outpatient has many risks and reasons for failure. Reasons for lack of success include but are not limited to overuse (or substance use on top of the prescribed taper), diversion/selling/trading of controlled substances, incorrect use by patients, and incorrect dosing by physicians. Required doses of benzodiazepines needed to replace alcohol and benzodiazepines cannot be known by the prescribing physician at the time of prescription as patients may have different requirements even at the same level of use. Symptoms of withdrawal must be allowed to progress so that appropriate and targeted doses of GABA replacement therapy with benzodiazepines can be delivered. The practice of prescribing tapered doses stil exists, though this is less appropriate than hospitalization for medical detoxification.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's chronic bingeing and purging behavior meets the criteria for bulimia nervosa. Bulimia nervosa is characterized by cycles of uncontrollable binge eating and compensatory behaviors intended to prevent weight gain (eg, vomiting, laxative misuse) that occur at least once weekly over a period of 3 months or more.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Bulimia nervosa is an eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of uncontrolled eating followed by compensatory behaviors, occurring at least once weekly for 3 months or longer.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Patients with the bingeing/purging type of anorexia or bulimia nervosa can show parotid gland enlargement, dental caries from gastric hydrochloric acid erosion of, enamel, scars on the knuckles secondary to abrasions from the incisors when inducing vomiting, and abdominal striae caused by abdominal overdistension from binge eating. The pathogenesis of bulimia nervosa is not fully understood, but both biological and psychosocial factors play a role. Genetic influences account for a significant portion of risk, and childhood adversity, such as this patient's parents divorcing, markedly increases susceptibility.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the precise cause of bulimia nervosa is unknown, there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of bulimia nervosa is through a combined medical and psychiatric approach and involves correcting fluid and electrolyte derangements alongside psychotherapy and fluoxetine therapy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Unlike patients with anorexia nervosa, patients with bulimia nervosa typically have a normal BMI. In this vignette, the patient is having difficulty adjusting to her parents' divorce. However, this adjustment difficulty manifests as eating disorder behaviors rather than anxiety or depressive symptoms (eg, adjustment disorder, persistent depressive disorder). She has a history of a suicide attempt, though this history is not specific to a particular psychiatric diagnosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Adjustment disorder is incorrect, Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of an emotional disturbance (eg, anxiety, depression, conduct disturbance) within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. In this vignette, the patient is having difficulty adjusting to her parents' divorce. However, this adjustment difficulty manifests as eating disorder behaviors rather than anxiety, depressive, or other behavioral symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Antisocial personality disorder is incorrect. Antisocial personality disorder is a cluster B (emotional or dramatic) personality disorder. It arises from a deficit in empathy, resulting in pervasive violations of others' rights, aggression, and a hostile and manipulative attitude toward others. This patient does not provide evidence of repeatedly violating others' rights.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Borderline personality disorder is incorrect. Borderline personality disorder is a cluster B personality disorder that features an unstable sense of self and tumultuous relationships. Though suicide attempts are common, they are not specific to borderline personality disorder.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Histrionic personality disorder is incorrect. Histrionic personality disorder, a cluster B personality disorder, is characterized by theatrical, superficial expressions of 'emotion that unconsciously serve to garner attention from others to fulfill one's emotional needs. This patient does not demonstrate theatrical expressions of emotion.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is incorrect. Persistent depressive disorder refers to at least three depression symptoms that have endured for at least 2 years. This patient does not present with depressive symptoms.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's evolving symptoms of dry mouth, dry eyes, hyperthermia, tachycardia, flushed skin, and confusion after receiving intramuscular chlorpromazine and diphenhydramine are consistent with anticholinergic toxicity as a result of the blockade of acetylcholine receptors.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Anticholinergic toxicity is a clinical diagnosis. Anticholinergic toxicity should be suspected in a patient experiencing symptoms, such as delirium, relative adrenergic/sympathomimetic excess (eg, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypertension, hyperthermia, tremors, agitation), dry mucous membranes, flushed skin without sweating, visual disturbance, ileus/constipation, and urinary retention, among others, following administration of medications known to block acetylcholine receptors.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Endogenous acetylcholine binding to muscarinic receptors causes smooth muscle contraction of the sweat glands, salivary glands, airways, bladder, and bowel. Cholinergic neurons projecting to the cerebral cortex mediate attention, learning, and memory. Antagonism of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors blocks cholinergic signaling in the brain and causes the dysfunction of smooth muscle contraction, leading to cognitive impairment, disorientation, delirium, sedation, mydriasis, anhidrosis (leading to mild hyperthermia and flushed skin in this patient), dry mouth, urinary retention, and/or constipation. Many medications block acetylcholine receptors, with some common examples including tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, and diphenhydramine.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Knowledge and avoidance of anticholinergic medications is the mainstay of anticholinergic toxicity prevention, though prevention may not always be possible. Patients may require administration of anticholinergic medications for their safety or for appropriate treatment, thus the benefits of administration may outweigh the risks. Patients receiving anticholinergic medications should be monitored for signs of anticholinergic toxidromes, which, if occurring, should also be treated.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of anticholinergic toxidrome includes discontinuing the offending medications and providing pharmacotherapy, such as benzodiazepines, which balance the state of relative sympathetic excess in anticholinergic toxicity. Supportive care with intravenous fluids, antipyretics, and sedatives is also appropriate depending on the severity of symptoms. In extreme cases, sedation with intubation may be required while the patient metabolizes the medication. Physostigmine therapy can be used in select cases; however, the appropriate dose to counterbalance the anticholinergic effect may be imprecise or difficult to calculate with accuracy, risking consequent cholinergic excess.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Understanding the action of neurotransmitters and symptoms resulting from relative excess or from blockade allows the narrowing of the differential to anticholinergic toxicity, which presents similarly to sympathomimetic toxicity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dopamine is not the most likely explanation for this patient's current symptoms. Blockade of dopamine improves psychotic symptoms, such as those seen in schizophrenia, and is appropriate in the management of acute psychosis. However, blockade of dopamine is associated with movement disorders, such as acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia, not anticholinergic excess.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Glutamate is not the most likely explanation for this patient's current symptoms. Blockade of glutamate is an effect of several medications, such as ketamine (a commonly misused dissociative anesthetic) and memantine (used in the management of dementia). Multiple varied symptoms result from glutamate blockade, including hallucinations and somnolence. However, the combination of dry mucous membranes, hyperadrenergic vital signs, and delirium after receiving diphenhydramine therapy is consistent with anticholinergic toxicity.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Histamine is incorrect. Blockade of histamine causes sedation and improves symptoms of allergy and histamine excess, such as itching, urticaria, and angioedema.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Serotonin is incorrect. Serotonin syndrome, which presents with some features common to anticholinergic toxicity (eg, increased body temperature, hypertension, tachycardia, tachypnea, and delirium), can result from increased serotonin release or blockade of serotonin uptake. Serotonin syndrome does not result from blockade of the serotonin receptor but rather from increased serotonin signaling.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has panic disorder, which is characterized by recurrent, unexpected panic attacks as well as at least a 1- month history of worry about future panic attacks or avoidance of panic attack triggers. First-line treatment typically involves selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), such as sertraline.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Panic disorder is characterized by recurrent panic attacks that are unexpected, such as this patient's experience when entering numerous classrooms. Panic disorder typically involves worry about future panic attacks or maladaptive behavior changes used to avoid panic attack triggers. Avoidance of panic attack triggers may lead to agoraphobia, the fear of leaving home. This patient's fear and refusal to attend school are a means of avoiding panic triggers, and during episodes she has described feeling symptoms of tachycardia, dizziness, and a fear of dying.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Panic attacks feature acute fear or anxiety that peaks within minutes and is associated with four or more of the following physical symptoms or associated mental states: heart palpitations or tachycardia, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, the sensation of choking, gastrointestinal distress, paresthesias, sweating, chills, trembling, derealization, the fear of dying, and/or the fear of losing control.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the cause of panic disorder is multifactorial (ie, involving various genetic and environmental triggers), there is no universal prevention strategy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment typically includes psychotherapy, long-term SSRI (eg, sertraline) therapy, and short-term benzodiazepine therapy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Other anxiety disorders may involve panic attacks secondary to a patient's anxiety. However, only panic disorder involves fear of future panic attacks and avoidance of panic attack triggers.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Bupropion is incorrect. Bupropion is an atypical antidepressant medication that increases synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine. Bupropion is commonly used in depression but less commonly helpful for anxiety, as it can lead to increased anxiety and restlessness as an adverse effect.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Hydroxyzine is incorrect. Hydroxyzine is an antihistamine medication used off-label as an as-needed anxiolytic. Hydroxyzine can be useful as an adjunctive medication for panic attacks, but SSRIs are evidence-based first-line treatments.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Propranolol is incorrect. Propranolol is a B-adrenergic blocking agent used to treat performance anxiety and antipsychotic-induced akathisia. This patient is preoccupied with the possibility of future panic attacks, not her performance.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Quetiapine is incorrect. Quetiapine is an atypical antipsychotic that is sometimes useful in refractory anxiety disorders. However, its adverse effect profile, including increased risk for metabolic syndrome, prohibits its use as a first-line agent for anxiety disorders.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient with stage IV pulmonary carcinoma expresses concern about uncontrolled pain. Acetaminophen with codeine is appropriate for mild pain but reaches a ceiling effect at higher doses when treating severe pain. Morphine, an opiate, is commonly used to treat pain from end-stage cancer and is an appropriate replacement medication despite the patient's history of opiate use disorder.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Pain due to end-stage cancer is frequently severe and may manifest as bone pain, myalgias, abdominal pain, chest pain, or even widespread pain. Such pain can be challenging to treat.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> End-stage cancer pain is multifactorial and often involves direct invasion from cancer into anatomical structures, such as bones, muscles, or nerves. This pain is often severe and should be treated with opiate pain medication that can be titrated up to high morphine milliequivalents, usually under the direction of pain management or palliative care. In the treatment of end-stage cancer, the primary objective is comfort and quality of life, not long-term addiction prevention. Untreated severe pain can cause significant suffering, so opiate pain medication remains the standard of care even for patients with a history of opioid use disorder.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Pain is expected in the setting of cancer that has progressed to stage IV. Preventive measures against cancer development, such as avoiding smoking along with early aggressive treatment of lower stage cancer, are two means of prevention.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Aggressively treating pain due to end-stage cancer with opiate pain medication is the most appropriate approach.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient reports pain as his major problem, which should be addressed by switching to an opiate pain medication. His mood has improved with nortriptyline, and further titration of medications for mood would not be effective. NSAIDs, such as naproxen, are inferior to opiates in the treatment of severe end-stage cancer pain.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Add aripiprazole therapy is incorrect. An antipsychotic would not appropriately address this patient's severe pain. His mood is improved on a higher dose of nortriptyline as evidenced by the fact that he is no longer suicidal.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Add naproxen therapy is not the most appropriate next step in management. Treating pain with end-stage cancer requires the use of opiate pain medications with high morphine milliequivalent. NSAIDs may be a reasonable adjunctive therapy to treat mild-to-moderate cancer pain, but the combination of naproxen with acetaminophen/codeine is not appropriate for severe pain.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Replace nortriptyline with duloxetine is incorrect. The patient's mood is improving on the higher dose of nortriptyline. Switching to duloxetine would not address his uncontrolled pain.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Continue current medication regimen is incorrect. Uncontrolled pain in the setting of end-stage cancer should be treated aggressively, and his current medication regimen is not accomplishing this.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presents with pressured speech, impulsive and inappropriate behavior, delusions of grandeur, and tremor, which are consistent with a presentation of mania. Her symptoms may be worsened by or directly induced by methylphenidate, which should be discontinued.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Episodes of mania can occur primarily or be induced by stimulants or certain antidepressant medications. Stimulants for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, such as methylphenidate, increase presynaptic release of dopamine and norepinephrine, which improve hyperactivity and inattention but can provoke mania.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> This patient demonstrates delusions of grandeur, goal-directed activity, pressured speech, and signs of stimulant intoxication (eg, tachycardia and tremor). Her presentation is consistent with substance-induced mania. Intoxication with stimulant drugs (eg, cocaine, methamphetamine, methylphenidate, caffeine) can lead to manic symptoms secondary to an increase in synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine. Other signs of stimulant intoxication include elevated or irritable mood, pressured speech, tachycardia, hypertension, and additional signs of sympathetic excess (eg, diaphoresis). She is more likely to experience substance-induced mania because of her existing diagnosis of bipolar disorder. The combination of methylphenidate and fluoxetine may also increase her risk for manic symptoms.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Avoidance of stimulant use in patients with bipolar disorder, especially if not paired with a mood stabilizer, is key in the prevention of manic episodes. Patients with bipolar disorder should also be monitored after initiation of or increasing dosage of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors as such medication changes can precipitate manic episodes.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Acute manic episodes induced by medication should be treated with discontinuation of the offending medication. Additionally, incapacitated patients should be admitted and stabilized with pharmacotherapy (eg, mood stabilizers, benzodiazepines, antipsychotics) for their safety and for the safety of others until the manic episode resolves.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Manic episodes can occur primarily or secondary to substance use or co-presenting medical or surgical condition. In this patient's case, her use of stimulants and 'examination findings are suggestive of excess stimulant use and narrows the causative differential to stimulant-induced mania.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Decrease the dosage of valproic acid is incorrect. Valproic acid is a mood stabilizer used in the management of bipolar disorder. Decreasing its dosage would worsen the symptoms of mania.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Increase the dosage of eszopiclone is incorrect. Eszopiclone is a nonbenzodiazepine sleep ald that does not have a role in the management of mania.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Increase the dosage of fluoxetine is incorrect. Fluoxetine, while often used in the management of depression, anxiety, and, in certain instances, bipolar disorder, can worsen symptoms of mania with increased dosing.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Initiate lithium carbonate is not the most appropriate next step in pharmacotherapy. Initiating lithium carbonate may be helpful in the management of bipolar disorder; however, precipitating or causative medications should first be discontinued. Lithium carbonate has a narrow therapeutic window and multiple adverse effects. It should be initiated only if safer measures have failed.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, with a history of disruptive and aggressive behavior with frequent physical and emotional outbursts, requires psychiatric stabilization in an inpatient setting given his dangerous and escalating behaviors. Frequent disruptive and aggressive behavior with chronic irritability and anger is consistent with disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (DMDD). The most appropriate next step is admittance to the psychiatric unit.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Frequent anger and temper outbursts, irritability, and difficulty functioning due to anger and irritability occurring in multiple settings are consistent with DMDD. Evaluation of the condition requires a thorough history demonstrating symptoms dating back more than 12 months, and exclusion of mimicking or triggering medical, psychiatric, or substance use disorder conditions.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Occurring in children and adolescents, DMDD is characterized by recurrent, severe anger and temper outbursts that may be verbal or physical, occurring on average three or more times weekly, regularly for at least 12 months. Patients generally demonstrate chronic anger and irritability. Anger outbursts and irritability are out of proportion to the triggering situation. Symptoms are demonstrated in two or more settings (eg, at home and at school). Symptoms of DMDD must occur in absence of manic, hypomanic, depressive, or psychotic symptoms, and must cause impairment in functioning at home, at school, with family, or with friends to be diagnosed. While symptoms generally begin before age 10 years, DMDD cannot be diagnosed before age 6 years or after age 18 years.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no specific preventive measure for DMDD.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Cognitive behavioral therapy and psychotherapy remain the mainstay of treatment. There is no specific targeted or definitively used pharmacotherapy. Parent training to assist in the response to irritable and angry behavior and to anticipate and avoid triggers to outbursts can be helpful. In cases of severe dysregulation leading to threats toward others or to the patient, as seen in this patient, inpatient hospitalization is appropriate.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's chronic history of anger, outbursts, impulsivity, and violent behavior narrows the differential to severe DMDD. Outpatient management has been unsuccessful, which necessitates inpatient management in this case.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Contact child protective services is incorrect. There is no evidence of any threat to the child imposed by his family, friends, school, or living situation. His family has been appropriately concerned and is taking steps to keep him, and themselves, safe.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Reinforce the importance of getting on a waiting list for outpatient care is incorrect. This family has already taken steps to obtain outpatient care, and there is no sign of outpatient management being available when needed. Furthermore, the child's behavior has been escalating and dangerous to himself and others, necessitating inpatient management.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Schedule an emergency appointment with the patient's pediatrician in the morning is incorrect. The patient's pediatrician is not the best practitioner to address the child's or family's needs, which require the involvement of psychiatrists and therapists. Waiting until the morning will further delay care and allow persistence of an unstable and dangerous situation that should only be expected to continue without intervention.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Begin family therapy is incorrect. Family therapy is appropriate in this child's case; however, it does not address the immediate safety concerns faced by the patient and his family. Inpatient admission for initial stabilization is the most appropriate next step.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute, uncomplicated grief (ie, bereavement) can manifest with sadness, shock, anguish, guilt, regret, difficulty concentrating, decreased interest in usual activities, preoccupation with thoughts and memories of the deceased, and neurovegetative symptoms. It is common for bereavement to recur with reminders of the deceased, such as anniversaries or holidays.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Acute bereavement presents in diverse ways. Manifestations can include sadness, shock, anguish, guilt, regret, difficulty concentrating, decreased interest in usual activities, preoccupation with thoughts and memories of the deceased, and neurovegetative symptoms (ie, decreased energy, sleep disturbance, appetite disturbance).</p><p><b>Definition:</b> In some patients, these symptoms can endure for months, though acute bereavement is expected to resolve within 6 months. Longer durations may signal the emergence of prolonged grief disorder. However, it is common for bereavement to recur with reminders of the deceased, such as anniversaries or holidays.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no universal way to prevent grief. Grief after a loss is natural and expected to improve with time.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management includes reassurance and normalization of her reaction (eg, \"It is common to feel the way you do\"), frequent reassessment for the development of suicidality and psychiatric disorders, such as major depressive disorder, and encouraging reconnection with family. In severe cases, the physician can facilitate referral for grief counseling.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Major depressive disorder should be excluded. In bereavement reactions, the criteria for major depressive disorder are not met.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Adjustment disorder with depressed mood is incorrect. Adjustment disorder with depressed mood refers to depressive symptoms that are out of proportion to a stressor and impair daily functioning. This patient is well groomed and able to complete her daily activities without difficulty. As well, her sadness is an understandable response to reminders of her husband's death.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Major depressive disorder is incorrect. Major depressive disorder involves 2 or more weeks of at least five of the following symptoms: depressed mood, anhedonia, feelings of guilt or worthlessness, difficulty concentrating, psychomotor retardation, suicidal thoughts, and/or neurovegetative symptoms. This patient does not meet symptom number or duration criteria for major depressive disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Mood disorder due to cerebrovascular disease is incorrect. Mood disorder due to cerebrovascular disease involves mood changes (eg, depression) caused directly by cerebrovascular pathology, such as stroke, rather than by psychosocial factors. This patient does not meet full mood disorder criteria and does not disclose focal neurologic symptoms consistent with cerebral infarction.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Post-traumatic stress disorder is incorrect. Post-traumatic stress disorder refers to symptoms of intrusive thoughts/memories of trauma, hyperarousal/fear, avoidance of trauma reminders, and/or negative mood/thoughts resulting from a traumatic event (eg, a life-threatening motor vehicle collision) and lasting more than 1 month. This patient has been thinking of her husband but does not describe intrusive/unwanted memories and is not avoidant of reminders (eg, she is able to attend the doctor's appointment despite being reminded). Also, she does not describe hyperarousal or fear.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient is developing delirium tremens, a serious complication of alcohol withdrawal, evidenced by delirium, hallucinations, and autonomic instability that typically develops 2 to 4 days after alcohol cessation. Administration of chlorpromazine is contraindicated in this patient, as it prolongs the QT interval and can mask his ongoing symptoms of withdrawal.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Alcohol withdrawal can occur within 6 to 12 hours of cessation of use. Seizures, autonomic instability (ie, hypertension, tachycardia), and delirium tremens can be life-threatening consequences. Diagnosis is based on clinical assessment.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Alcohol increases the central nervous system activity of y-aminobutyric acid (GABA), the main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Abrupt discontinuation of alcohol leads to sympathetic excess. Uncomplicated alcohol withdrawal typically presents with tremor, anxiety, restlessness, headache, nausea, and diaphoresis. Delirium tremens, the most concerning and life-threatening complication, involves severe confusion and disorientation, fluctuations in consciousness, agitation, visual hallucinations, and autonomic instability (Ie, fluctuations in heart rate and blood pressure with hyperthermia), which is seen in this patient. Patients with alcohol use disorder often have significant fluid and electrolyte imbalances (eg, prolonged QT interval in this patient) in addition to vitamin deficiencies.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Avoidance of substance use prevents progression to withdrawal. For patients with existing heavy alcohol use, GABA replacement therapy should occur to treat or prevent withdrawal.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Alcohol withdrawal is treated with replacement therapy, generally with benzodiazepines, followed by a gradual taper over days to weeks. Fluid and electrolyte derangements should be corrected, and vitamin repletion, especially vitamin B1 (thiamine), folic acid, and magnesium, should occur. Long QT syndrome must be corrected, which is usually a function of hypomagnesemia and/or hypokalemia, and medications that prolong the QT interval should be avoided (eg, chlorpromazine). Regardless, this patient's hallucinations and delirium should first be managed with agents that enhance GABA activity (eg, benzodiazepines), not with antipsychotic medications as they can mask 'ongoing symptoms of withdrawal (eg, seizures) and delay appropriate intervention.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's history of heavy alcohol use with abrupt cessation in the setting of surgery is the precursor to alcohol withdrawal. Many medications are appropriate to manage alcohol withdrawal, including benzodiazepines and vitamin/electrolyte repletion. Medications that prolong the QT interval are contraindicated, such as chlorpromazine.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Clonidine is not contraindicated in this patient. Clonidine is used in the management of opioid withdrawal and hypertension. While clonidine is not contraindicated in long QT syndrome, it is also not an appropriate first choice in the management of sympathetic overactivity in alcohol withdrawal. GABA replacement therapy with benzodiazepines is the most appropriate treatment.</p><p><b>Choice C and Choice E:</b> Folic acid and vitamin B1 (thiamine) are not contraindicated in this patient. Folic acid and vitamin B1 replacement are appropriate in alcohol use disorder and alcohol withdrawal as patients are often deficient. Folic acid and vitamin B1 replacement are indicated and do not affect the QT interval like chlorpromazine.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Lorazepam is not contraindicated in this patient. Lorazepam therapy is appropriate in alcohol withdrawal, as it is a benzodiazepine and directly increases central nervous system GABA. Lorazepam does not prolong the QT interval like chlorpromazine.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Benzodiazepines such as temazepam are known to cause delirium in older adults, especially those with underlying dementia. This patient, who presents with fatigue, confusion, forgetfulness, ataxia, dysarthria, and disorientation, is likely experiencing temazepam intoxication after its recent initiation.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Benzodiazepines are commonly used for anxiety and insomnia. However, high doses, use in patients who may slowly metabolize medications (eg, hepatic/renal disease), use in patients taking other sedatives or antipsychotic medication, or use in older persons or those with underlying dementia can result in adverse effects, such as confusion/delirium and symptoms of benzodiazepine intoxication. The diagnosis is clinical after exclusion of competing differential diagnoses.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Benzodiazepines act on the central nervous system to increase y-aminobutyric acid, the primary central inhibitory neurotransmitter. The effect of benzodiazepines in overdose or intoxication includes cognitive slowing, sedation, and cerebellar symptoms, such as impaired coordination, dysarthria/slurred speech, and ataxia. Patient examination may disclose bradycardia and hypotension, though such symptoms are less common. The adverse effects of benzodiazepines can be more severe and their on-target effects more exaggerated in persons at risk, such as older individuals, those with dementia, those with impaired clearance, or those on medications that could increase or augment the effect. For this reason, benzodiazepines are often contraindicated in such settings.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Avoidance/limitation of medications that provoke delirium in older persons or those with dementia is appropriate. These include benzodiazepines, opioids, anticholinergics, and sedatives/sleep aids, among others.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Discontinuation of offending medications is the first step in management of medication-induced delirium. Patients should then be managed in a safe environment until their mental status and functional status return to baseline.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The occurrence of this patient's symptoms following initiation of a known delirium-triggering medication makes the most likely differential diagnosis medication-related delirium and intoxication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cerebral infarction is not the most likely diagnosis. Cerebral infarction can present with ataxia and dysarthria; however, this patient's symptoms of delirium, fatigue, and confusion make a global cerebral process, as opposed to a focal infarction, more likely. The time course of symptoms starting after initiation of temazepam also argues for a medication-induced process.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dementia, Alzheimer type, is incorrect. Dementia, Alzheimer type, presents with slow and gradual cognitive decline and loss of executive function, without focal neurologic deficits and not necessarily following initiation of a sedating medication.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Mult-infarct (vascular) dementia is incorrect. Vascular dementia presents with a stepwise cognitive and functional decline after multiple large or small cerebral infarctions, of which this patient does not have history. His only history is myocardial infarction, which does not cause dementia.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Normal aging is incorrect. While some cognitive decline can occur with normal aging, this patient has had an acute and significant change from his normal high- functioning baseline, occurring after initiation of a triggering medication. This pattern is not consistent with normal aging.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: It is normal behavior for a 10-year-old girl to prefer to talk with her mother about puberty and gender-specific developmental needs. However, it is also important to validate the father's concerns and encourage him to find other ways to remain involved in his daughter's life while respecting her wishes for modesty regarding her changing body.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Adolescents may prefer to confide in caregivers of the same sex and/or gender regarding pubertal issues and needs, though preferences vary by individual and family dynamic.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Adolescents often experience behavioral changes during puberty, which may include preferring to talk with one caregiver about sensitive topics, such as menstruation, breast development, and behavioral changes. Some caregivers may share lived experiences that make these conversations feel more comfortable. Respecting an adolescent's desire for modesty and privacy is important. Disregarding these preferences can create tension and strain relationships.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In this case, itis important to validate the father's concerns while suggesting options to continue to be involved in his daughter's life. A history of psychological issues, such as an absentee father in childhood, can make it difficult for the father to navigate the situation. Attempts to pathologize the daughter's decisions to confide in her mother about these issues could harm family relationships and should be avoided.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Contact child protective services for an evaluation of the family is incorrect. There is no evidence of abuse in this family dynamic. The daughter's behavior is normal child behavior and the father's concerns are understandable given his personal history.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Offer to speak with his wife about the important role that fathers play in their daughters' development is incorrect. While it may be beneficial for the father to talk with his wife about his concerns, it would not be appropriate for the physician to mediate the situation, and it risks patronizing the wife or limiting her own relationship with her daughter.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Recommend that he undergo psychotherapy to learn to deal with his feelings about his father is not the most appropriate next step. Although the patient would likely benefit from being able to talk about his issues with his own father, this recommendation does not directly address the patient's concerns and unfairly pathologizes his feelings about the situation.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Suggest that he attend family therapy with his wife and daughter is not the most appropriate next step. The desire for privacy in the daughter is normal child behavior and family therapy may unintentionally stigmatize her desire for privacy. While the result still may lead to opportunities for the father to remain in the daughter's life while respecting her privacy, this strategy may lead to more conflict.</p>"};
